/* FMT 9 (contd): arsenic, lead, corrosives, narcotics, plant & cardiac poisons, hydrocarbons */
Z("fmt9",5,"f",["Arsenic (commonly arsenic trioxide — white arsenic, 'sankhya') is a metallic irritant poison that inhibits sulfhydryl enzymes (pyruvate dehydrogenase) and uncouples oxidative phosphorylation; acute poisoning mimics cholera (rice-water stools, vomiting, dehydration, shock), while chronic poisoning (groundwater in West Bengal/Bangladesh, homicidal small doses) produces raindrop pigmentation, palmoplantar hyperkeratosis, Mees' lines in nails, peripheral neuropathy and cancers; arsenic is ideal for homicide (tasteless, odourless, cheap), retards putrefaction, and is detectable in hair, nails and bones years after death; BAL/DMSA are antidotes.","Arsenic poisons the enzymes every cell needs; one big dose looks like cholera, small repeated doses mark the skin, nails and nerves over months.",["Fatal dose 120–200 mg As₂O₃; fatal period 12–48 h (acute); arsine gas (AsH₃) causes haemolysis.","Acute (gastroenteric type): metallic taste, burning throat, vomiting, colicky pain, profuse watery 'rice-water' stools with blood/mucus, tenesmus, cramps, dehydration, oliguria, shock, QT prolongation; garlic odour; vs cholera — throat pain, tenesmus, stool with blood, conjunctivitis, arsenic in stool; paralytic/narcotic type (massive dose — CNS collapse).","Chronic: pigmentation (raindrop, 'rain drops on dusty road'), hyperkeratosis of palms and soles, Mees' lines (transverse white bands in nails — Aldrich–Mees), alopecia, conjunctivitis, coryza, sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy (glove-stocking), anaemia, hepatomegaly, non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis, blackfoot disease (Taiwan), skin/lung/bladder cancers (Bowen's disease).","Treatment: lavage (with egg albumin/milk), activated charcoal poorly effective, fluids, electrolytes; chelation — BAL (dimercaprol) 3–5 mg/kg IM 4-hourly, DMSA (succimer) oral, DMPS; dialysis for renal failure; arsine — exchange transfusion.","PM: acute — red velvety gastric mucosa with submucous haemorrhages and white particles adherent ('red velvet'), subendocardial haemorrhages, fatty liver, delayed putrefaction (mummified/preserved bodies); preserve hair (whole length with roots), nails, bones (exhumation), viscera; Marsh, Reinsch tests; neutron activation; medicolegal — homicidal (ideal poison), cattle poison, accidental (pesticides, groundwater, ayurvedic bhasmas), abortifacient."],"Acute arsenic mimics cholera; chronic — raindrop pigmentation, Mees' lines, neuropathy; BAL."],
"Arsenic is a metalloid; the poisonous compounds are arsenious oxide/arsenic trioxide (As₂O₃ — white arsenic, 'sankhya' or 'somalkar' in India — white, tasteless, odourless, sparingly soluble powder — the commonest homicidal arsenical), copper arsenite (Scheele's green), copper acetoarsenite (Paris green), arsenic sulphides (orpiment — yellow, 'hartal'; realgar — red, 'mansil'), sodium/potassium arsenite (Fowler's solution, sheep dips, weed killers), lead and calcium arsenate (insecticides), organic arsenicals (arsenobenzol, lewisite — war gas), arsine gas (AsH₃ — industrial); sources of exposure — pesticides, rodenticides, herbicides, wood preservatives, glass and semiconductor industry, contaminated groundwater (natural geological arsenic in tube wells — West Bengal, Bihar, UP, Assam and Bangladesh — the largest mass poisoning in history), traditional (Ayurvedic) medicines and bhasmas, cattle poisoning, homicidal poisoning. Mechanism: trivalent arsenic (arsenite) binds to sulfhydryl (–SH) groups of enzymes (especially lipoic acid of pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase), inhibiting cellular respiration and glucose metabolism; pentavalent arsenate substitutes for phosphate (arsenolysis), uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation; capillary damage (dilatation, increased permeability — fluid loss into GIT); arsine causes massive haemolysis; chronic — oxidative stress, DNA damage, carcinogenesis (IARC Group 1). Fatal dose — 120–200 mg of arsenic trioxide; fatal period — 12–48 hours (acute); arsenic is absorbed from GIT, lungs, skin; distributed to liver, kidneys, then deposited in keratin-rich tissues (hair, nails, skin) within 2 weeks and in bones; excreted in urine (main), faeces, sweat, milk. Acute poisoning: (1) gastroenteric (most common) form — symptoms begin 30 min–1 h after ingestion (delayed with food): sweetish metallic taste, burning pain in throat and oesophagus, dysphagia, intense thirst, nausea, persistent vomiting (blood-tinged), severe colicky abdominal pain, profuse watery diarrhoea resembling rice-water stools (with blood and mucus, tenesmus) — 'cholera-like', dehydration, cramps in calves, oliguria/anuria, cold clammy skin, hypotension, shock, cardiac arrhythmias (QT prolongation, torsades), ARDS, encephalopathy, convulsions, coma, death within 1–3 days; survivors develop peripheral neuropathy (2–3 weeks), skin changes and Mees' lines (~ 6 weeks); garlic odour of breath; (2) narcotic (paralytic) form — with massive doses: rapid collapse, CNS depression, coma, death within hours with little GI symptoms; (3) arsine gas poisoning — haemolysis (dark red urine — haemoglobinuria), jaundice, abdominal pain, acute renal failure; differences between acute arsenic poisoning and cholera — in arsenic: burning pain in throat before vomiting, vomiting precedes purging, stools with blood and mucus, tenesmus, conjunctivitis, voice not lost, cramps less, arsenic found in vomit/stools; in cholera: no throat pain, purging precedes vomiting, rice-water stools without blood, no tenesmus, voice husky/lost (vox cholerica), severe cramps, Vibrio cholerae in stools. Chronic poisoning (repeated small doses — homicidal, occupational, groundwater): (1) skin — diffuse or 'raindrop' hyperpigmentation (finely freckled spots of dark pigment interspersed with depigmented spots — 'raindrops on a dusty road'), arsenical keratosis (hyperkeratosis) of palms and soles (warty papules/corns), exfoliative dermatitis, Bowen's disease, squamous and basal cell carcinoma; (2) nails — Mees' lines (Aldrich–Mees lines — transverse white bands across the nail plate, appearing about 4–6 weeks after exposure, move distally with nail growth (~ 0.1 mm/day) — help date exposure), brittle nails; (3) hair — alopecia; arsenic deposited in hair (segmental analysis gives timeline of exposure); (4) nervous system — symmetrical sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy (glove-and-stocking paraesthesiae, burning feet, weakness, foot drop — Guillain–Barré-like), encephalopathy; (5) mucosa — conjunctivitis, coryza, laryngitis, stomatitis; (6) GIT — anorexia, nausea, diarrhoea, weight loss; liver — hepatomegaly, non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis (portal hypertension); (7) blood — anaemia, leucopenia, basophilic stippling; (8) cardiovascular — peripheral vascular disease (blackfoot disease — Taiwan), hypertension; (9) cancers — skin, lung, bladder, kidney, liver (angiosarcoma); (10) diabetes. Diagnosis: 24-hour urinary arsenic (> 50 µg/L; speciation to exclude non-toxic seafood arsenobetaine), blood arsenic (acute), hair and nail arsenic (chronic — > 1 mg/kg hair), X-ray abdomen (radio-opaque arsenic in GIT), ECG, renal and liver function, nerve conduction studies; laboratory tests — Reinsch test (copper strip in acidified sample turns grey/black), Marsh test (arsine gas → silver mirror), Gutzeit test, atomic absorption spectroscopy, ICP-MS, neutron activation analysis (hair — Napoleon studies). Treatment: (1) acute — gastric lavage (warm water, with egg albumin/milk as demulcent; freshly prepared ferric hydroxide — historical antidote: 'hydrated ferric oxide' forms insoluble ferric arsenite), whole bowel irrigation if radio-opaque material in gut; activated charcoal (poor binding); aggressive IV fluids and electrolytes (cholera-like losses), vasopressors, cardiac monitoring (avoid QT-prolonging drugs); (2) chelation — BAL (British anti-lewisite, dimercaprol) 3–5 mg/kg deep IM every 4–6 h for 2 days, then 12-hourly for 7–10 days; DMSA (succimer — oral, 10 mg/kg 8-hourly) or DMPS (unithiol — IV/oral) after stabilisation; D-penicillamine (less preferred); haemodialysis in renal failure; (3) arsine — exchange transfusion, haemodialysis (BAL ineffective); (4) chronic — removal from source (safe water — arsenic-free tube wells, filters), nutrition, chelation of limited benefit, treat complications, cancer surveillance. Post-mortem findings: (A) acute — signs of dehydration (sunken eyes, wrinkled skin), cyanosis; rigor mortis may be prolonged; stomach — mucosa intensely red, swollen, velvety ('red velvet appearance'), with submucous haemorrhages (petechiae), excess mucus; white particles of arsenic adherent to the mucosal folds (especially greater curvature and posterior wall), occasionally ulceration; small intestine — inflamed, with rice-water contents; subendocardial haemorrhages (left ventricle, septum — characteristic), fatty degeneration of liver, heart, kidneys; cerebral oedema; (B) chronic — skin pigmentation, keratosis, Mees' lines, emaciation, fatty liver, neuropathy; (C) arsenic retards putrefaction (antiseptic) — bodies may be well preserved or mummified for long periods; (D) samples — stomach and contents, intestines, liver, kidney, blood, urine; in chronic and exhumation cases — hair (whole length, plucked with roots — tied), nails (all), bones (long bones), skin; soil samples from grave (control) in exhumation; arsenic persists for years and is detectable even in skeletal remains. Medicolegal aspects: (1) homicidal poison par excellence (historically the commonest homicidal poison — 'inheritance powder'): cheap, easily available, tasteless and odourless, white powder easily mixed with food (sugar, flour, milk, pan), small doses not suspected, symptoms resemble natural disease (gastroenteritis, cholera, neuritis), can be given in repeated small doses (slow poisoning); disadvantages — detectable in tissues for years (hair, nails, bones), even after burial/cremation (bones); (2) accidental — groundwater contamination (endemic arsenicosis), occupational, contaminated food, pesticide misuse, traditional medicines (Ayurvedic/Chinese preparations), mistaken for flour/sugar; (3) suicidal — less common; (4) cattle poisoning (to collect hides — arsenic mixed in fodder — 'rasaut'); (5) as an abortifacient (paste on abortion stick), aphrodisiac, hair remover; tolerance in 'arsenic eaters' (Styrian peasants); (6) legal — S. 123 BNS (hurt by poison), murder; Poisons Act; Drugs and Cosmetics Act (Ayurvedic preparations — heavy metal limits).",
[["Acute arsenic poisoning vs cholera",[["Feature","Arsenic","Cholera"],["Throat pain","Burning, before vomiting","Absent"],["Order","Vomiting precedes purging","Purging precedes vomiting"],["Stools","Rice-water with blood, mucus; tenesmus","Rice-water, no blood, no tenesmus"],["Conjunctivitis","Present","Absent"],["Voice","Normal","Husky/lost"],["Cramps","Less","Severe"],["Lab","Arsenic in vomit/stool","Vibrio cholerae"]]],
["Chronic arsenic poisoning",[["System","Features"],["Skin","Raindrop pigmentation, palmoplantar keratosis, Bowen's disease, cancers"],["Nails","Mees' lines (4–6 weeks)"],["Hair","Alopecia; arsenic deposition"],["Nerves","Glove-stocking sensorimotor neuropathy"],["Mucosa","Conjunctivitis, coryza"],["Liver","Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis"],["Vessels","Blackfoot disease"],["Cancers","Skin, lung, bladder, liver"]]],
["Treatment",["Lavage; IV fluids, electrolytes; cardiac monitoring","BAL 3–5 mg/kg IM 4–6 hourly","DMSA/DMPS orally after stabilisation","Dialysis in renal failure","Arsine: exchange transfusion","Chronic: safe water source"]],
["Post-mortem and medicolegal",["Red velvety gastric mucosa with adherent white particles","Subendocardial haemorrhages, fatty liver","Delayed putrefaction (preserved bodies)","Hair, nails, bones retain arsenic for years","Tests: Reinsch, Marsh, AAS, ICP-MS, neutron activation","Ideal homicidal poison; cattle poison; groundwater arsenicosis"]]],
{c:"Arsenic, chiefly white arsenic trioxide, poisons sulfhydryl enzymes, causing cholera-like gastroenteritis with shock in acute poisoning and raindrop pigmentation, palmoplantar keratosis, Mees' lines, neuropathy and cancers in chronic exposure; treated with fluids and BAL or DMSA, it leaves a red velvety stomach and delays putrefaction, and its persistence in hair, nails and bones makes it detectable long after death despite its reputation as the ideal homicidal poison."});

Z("fmt9",6,"f",["Lead poisoning (plumbism/saturnism) is usually chronic, from occupational exposure (battery, smelting, paints, printing), lead pipes, cosmetics (surma, sindoor), ayurvedic medicines and old paint (children — pica); lead inhibits δ-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD) and ferrochelatase (heme synthesis) and –SH enzymes, causing microcytic anaemia with basophilic stippling, Burton's blue line on gums, colic and constipation, peripheral motor neuropathy (wrist drop, foot drop), encephalopathy (children), nephropathy and gout; treated by removal from source and chelation with CaNa₂EDTA, DMSA, BAL and penicillamine.","Lead quietly replaces calcium and blocks heme-making enzymes — the clues are a blue gum line, stippled red cells, colic and wrist drop.",["Sources: lead-acid battery recycling, smelting, mining, plumbing, paints (white lead), printing, glazed pottery, petrol (tetraethyl lead — banned in India 2000), surma/kohl, sindoor, ayurvedic bhasmas, toys, bullets retained.","Features (chronic): facial pallor (earliest), anaemia (microcytic, hypochromic), basophilic stippling of RBCs, Burton's line (blue-black line on gum margin — lead sulphide), lead colic (severe periumbilical pain, constipation), palsy — motor neuropathy of most used muscles: wrist drop (radial nerve — extensor muscles), foot drop; encephalopathy (children > adults — convulsions, raised ICP, mental retardation), nephropathy (Fanconi, interstitial fibrosis), saturnine gout, hypertension, infertility, abortion, metallic taste; acute — rare (abdominal pain, vomiting, encephalopathy).","Investigations: blood lead (> 10 µg/dl abnormal; CDC reference 3.5 µg/dl children; > 70 µg/dl severe), raised urinary δ-ALA and coproporphyrin III, zinc protoporphyrin (free erythrocyte protoporphyrin) raised, basophilic stippling, X-ray — 'lead lines' at metaphyses (children — dense bands), radio-opaque flakes in gut.","Treatment: remove source; chelation — CaNa₂EDTA (IV 1 g/m²/day × 5 days; not Na₂EDTA — hypocalcaemia), BAL (with EDTA in encephalopathy; BAL first to prevent redistribution to brain), DMSA (succimer — oral, children), D-penicillamine; encephalopathy — mannitol, seizure control; calcium, iron.","Medicolegal: occupational disease (Factories Act — notifiable), accidental (children, cosmetics, medicines), homicidal and suicidal rare; abortifacient (lead plaster — diachylon paste on abortion stick); PM — Burton's line, pale organs, encephalopathy; bones store 90% of body lead (exhumation); preserve blood, bones, hair, viscera."],"Burton's line, basophilic stippling, wrist drop, colic — chelate with EDTA/BAL/DMSA."],
"Lead (Pb, 'saturn' of alchemists — 'saturnism', 'plumbism') is a heavy metal widely used in industry; poisoning is usually chronic and occupational or environmental; compounds — lead carbonate (white lead, 'safeda' — paints), lead oxide (litharge, red lead — 'sindoor' adulterant), lead acetate (sugar of lead — sweet taste), lead sulphide (galena — 'surma'), lead chromate, tetraethyl lead (organic — petrol antiknock, now banned). Sources: (1) occupational — lead mining, smelting, refining, lead-acid battery manufacture and informal battery recycling (major source in India), plumbing, soldering, welding/cutting lead-painted metal, painting (old paints), printing (type metal), glass and ceramics (glazes), cable sheathing, ammunition (shooting ranges), radiator repair, ship breaking; (2) environmental — lead-based paints in old houses (children with pica eating paint flakes, dust), contaminated soil and dust near smelters and recycling units, leaded petrol (historical — banned in India in 2000), lead pipes and storage tanks (soft acidic water), lead-glazed pottery and utensils (acidic food), contaminated spices (turmeric adulterated with lead chromate), toys and jewellery; (3) cosmetics and traditional products — surma/kajal/kohl (lead sulphide applied to infants' eyes), sindoor (red lead/vermilion), hair dyes; traditional (Ayurvedic, Unani, Chinese) medicines containing heavy metals (bhasmas, rasa preparations); (4) retained bullets/shrapnel (in joints — synovial fluid dissolves lead); (5) moonshine (illicit liquor distilled in radiators). Absorption — through lungs (fumes, dust — 30–50%), GIT (adults 10–15%; children 40–50% — more vulnerable, especially with iron/calcium deficiency), skin (organic lead — tetraethyl lead); distribution — blood (99% in RBCs; half-life ~ 30–35 days), soft tissues (liver, kidney, brain — half-life ~ 40 days), bones and teeth (90% of body burden in adults — half-life 20–30 years; mobilised in pregnancy, lactation, fractures, osteoporosis, acidosis, fever); crosses placenta; excreted in urine and bile. Mechanism: lead binds sulfhydryl groups and mimics calcium/zinc; (1) inhibits heme synthesis enzymes — δ-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD — most sensitive; δ-ALA accumulates in blood and urine) and ferrochelatase (iron not incorporated — zinc protoporphyrin/free erythrocyte protoporphyrin accumulates), coproporphyrinogen oxidase (coproporphyrin III in urine) → microcytic hypochromic anaemia (also haemolysis — inhibits pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase → aggregates of ribosomal RNA = basophilic stippling); (2) neurotoxicity — interferes with neurotransmitter release (calcium mimicry), damages capillaries (cerebral oedema), demyelination and axonal degeneration of peripheral motor nerves; (3) nephrotoxicity — proximal tubular damage (Fanconi syndrome, aminoaciduria, glycosuria, phosphaturia), chronic interstitial nephritis, reduced urate excretion (saturnine gout); (4) reproductive toxicity; (5) hypertension. Clinical features — chronic lead poisoning: (1) general — facial pallor (earliest and most consistent sign — due to vasoconstriction and anaemia; 'lead pallor'), weakness, fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, insomnia, headache, metallic (sweetish) taste, foul breath; (2) Burton's line (lead line) — a stippled blue-black line on the gum margin (gingival border adjacent to teeth, especially upper jaw/lower incisors) due to deposition of lead sulphide (formed by reaction of lead with hydrogen sulphide produced by bacterial action on food debris) — seen in ~ 50–70%, absent in edentulous and with good oral hygiene; (3) gastrointestinal — lead colic (severe, intermittent, periumbilical colicky abdominal pain, relieved by pressure — abdominal wall rigid but not tender; spasm of intestinal smooth muscle — may mimic acute abdomen), obstinate constipation (diarrhoea rarely), nausea, vomiting; (4) haematological — anaemia (microcytic, hypochromic; sometimes normocytic), basophilic stippling (punctate basophilia) of RBCs (characteristic but not specific — also in thalassaemia, sideroblastic anaemia), reticulocytosis; (5) neuromuscular — lead palsy (motor peripheral neuropathy — purely motor, no sensory loss), affecting the most active muscles first: extensors of the wrist and fingers (radial nerve — wrist drop — 'painter's palsy'; supinator longus spared), extensors of the ankle (foot drop), deltoid, biceps; fine tremors; muscle wasting; arthralgia, myalgia; (6) encephalopathy (lead encephalopathy — more common in children with blood lead > 70–100 µg/dl; in adults with organic lead) — headache, irritability, behavioural changes, ataxia, vomiting, visual disturbances, papilloedema (raised intracranial pressure — cerebral oedema), convulsions, delirium, coma, death; survivors — mental retardation, learning disabilities, behavioural problems; chronic low-level exposure in children causes reduced IQ, attention deficits, hearing loss, growth retardation (no safe level); (7) renal — chronic nephropathy, Fanconi syndrome, hypertension, saturnine gout; (8) reproductive — infertility, oligospermia, menstrual disorders, abortion, stillbirth, preterm labour; (9) eye — retinal stippling, optic neuritis; (10) others — 'lead lines' on X-ray of long bones in children (dense transverse bands at metaphyses — growing ends of femur, tibia, radius), hypothyroidism. Acute lead poisoning (rare — ingestion of lead acetate or other soluble salts, inhalation of fumes) — metallic taste, burning abdominal pain, vomiting (white — lead chloride), diarrhoea (black — lead sulphide) or constipation, shock, haemolysis, renal failure, encephalopathy, convulsions; fatal dose — lead acetate ~ 20–30 g; fatal period 2–3 days. Diagnosis: (1) blood lead level — the gold standard: acceptable reference < 5 µg/dl (adults), CDC blood lead reference value for children 3.5 µg/dl (2021) — no safe level; > 10–20 µg/dl — exposure; > 40 µg/dl (adults) — symptoms likely; > 70–80 µg/dl — severe, encephalopathy risk; (2) zinc protoporphyrin/free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (raised — reflects average exposure over 3–4 months), urinary δ-aminolevulinic acid (raised > 15 mg/L) and coproporphyrin III; (3) CBC — anaemia, basophilic stippling; (4) urinary lead (24-h; after EDTA mobilisation test — historical); (5) X-ray — lead lines in metaphyses (children), radio-opaque paint chips in abdomen; (6) bone lead by K-shell X-ray fluorescence (body burden); (7) hair lead (less reliable); (8) renal function, uric acid; nerve conduction studies; CT/MRI for encephalopathy. Treatment: (1) removal from the source of exposure (most important) — industrial hygiene, workplace changes, abatement of lead paint, safe water, stopping lead-containing cosmetics/medicines; (2) decontamination in acute ingestion — gastric lavage with magnesium sulphate or sodium sulphate (forms insoluble lead sulphate), whole bowel irrigation for radio-opaque material; (3) chelation therapy (based on blood lead level and symptoms): (a) CaNa₂EDTA (calcium disodium edetate — IV/IM 1000–1500 mg/m²/day for 5 days; mobilises lead from bone and soft tissues and excretes in urine; must use the calcium disodium salt — disodium EDTA causes fatal hypocalcaemia); in encephalopathy, EDTA is preceded (by 4 h) and accompanied by BAL to prevent redistribution of lead into the brain; (b) BAL (dimercaprol — IM 3–5 mg/kg 4-hourly; with EDTA in encephalopathy and BLL > 70 µg/dl); (c) DMSA (succimer — oral, 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for 5 days then 12-hourly for 14 days — preferred in children with BLL 45–69 µg/dl and in adults with moderate poisoning); (d) D-penicillamine (oral — second-line); repeat courses after rebound (redistribution from bone); (4) supportive — management of encephalopathy (ICU, mannitol, dexamethasone, anticonvulsants — benzodiazepines; avoid lumbar puncture in raised ICP), correction of iron, calcium and zinc deficiency (reduce absorption), calcium gluconate for colic (relieves spasm), analgesics, adequate nutrition, physiotherapy for palsy; (5) screening of family/co-workers; children follow-up for neurodevelopment. Post-mortem findings: chronic — pallor, emaciation, Burton's line, wasting of extensor muscles; pale organs, fatty degeneration; contracted granular kidneys; cerebral oedema with flattened gyri (encephalopathy); acute — gastrointestinal inflammation, dark contents; samples — blood (EDTA), urine, liver, kidney, brain, bones (store 90% of lead — long bone in exhumation), hair, teeth; analysis by atomic absorption spectroscopy, ICP-MS. Medicolegal aspects: (1) occupational disease — lead poisoning is a notifiable occupational disease under the Factories Act 1948 (S. 89, Third Schedule) and Employees' Compensation Act 1923 (Schedule III) — medical surveillance (periodic blood lead tests), compensation; (2) accidental poisoning — children (pica, paint, toys, surma), adulterated food/spices, traditional medicines, contaminated water; (3) suicidal and homicidal poisoning — rare (lead acetate occasionally); (4) as an abortifacient — lead pills ('diachylon pills' — lead oleate plaster) and lead paste on abortion sticks (lead poisoning in pregnant women); (5) public health — ban on leaded petrol (2000), limits on lead in paints (Regulation on Lead Contents in Household and Decorative Paints Rules 2016 — 90 ppm), BIS standards for water (10 µg/L), toys; (6) environmental justice — informal battery recycling communities; (7) gunshot victims with retained bullets — chronic lead toxicity.",
[["Sources of lead",["Battery manufacture/recycling, smelting, mining","Paints (white lead), printing, plumbing, glazes","Surma/kajal, sindoor, ayurvedic bhasmas","Lead pipes, contaminated spices (turmeric)","Leaded petrol (banned 2000)","Retained bullets"]],
["Clinical features of chronic lead poisoning",[["System","Features"],["General","Facial pallor (earliest), metallic taste, fatigue"],["Gums","Burton's blue line (lead sulphide)"],["GIT","Lead colic, constipation"],["Blood","Microcytic anaemia, basophilic stippling"],["Nerves","Motor neuropathy — wrist drop, foot drop"],["Brain","Encephalopathy (children), low IQ"],["Kidney","Fanconi syndrome, interstitial nephritis, saturnine gout, hypertension"],["Reproduction","Infertility, abortion, stillbirth"],["Bones (X-ray)","Lead lines at metaphyses (children)"]]],
["Investigations",[["Test","Finding"],["Blood lead","> 10 µg/dl abnormal; > 70 severe"],["ZPP/FEP","Raised (ferrochelatase block)"],["Urinary δ-ALA, coproporphyrin III","Raised (ALAD block)"],["Peripheral smear","Basophilic stippling"],["X-ray","Metaphyseal lead lines; radio-opaque flakes"]]],
["Chelation",[["Agent","Use"],["CaNa₂EDTA","IV; mainstay (never Na₂EDTA)"],["BAL","With EDTA in encephalopathy (given first)"],["DMSA (succimer)","Oral; children, moderate cases"],["D-penicillamine","Oral second-line"]]],
["Medicolegal",["Notifiable occupational disease (Factories Act)","Employees' Compensation Act","Accidental in children; cosmetics, medicines","Abortifacient (diachylon)","Bones store 90% — exhumation"]]],
{c:"Chronic lead poisoning from batteries, paints, cosmetics like surma and traditional medicines inhibits heme synthesis and sulfhydryl enzymes, producing pallor, microcytic anaemia with basophilic stippling, Burton's line, colic, wrist drop, nephropathy and childhood encephalopathy; it is diagnosed by blood lead and protoporphyrin levels, treated by removal from exposure and chelation with CaNa₂EDTA, BAL and DMSA, and is a notifiable occupational disease."});

Z("fmt9",7,"f",["Corrosives destroy tissues at the site of contact; sulphuric acid (oil of vitriol) causes coagulative necrosis with black charring (dehydration, carbonisation) of the mouth, oesophagus and stomach, severe pain, vomiting of coffee-ground material, perforation, shock and later strictures, and is used in vitriolage (acid attacks, S. 124 BNS); carbolic acid (phenol) is a protoplasmic poison that corrodes mucosa white then brown, anaesthetises it (little pain), causes CNS depression, coma, hypothermia and green-black urine (carboluria), and is treated with lavage using olive oil/glycerine (an exception to the lavage contraindication).","Sulphuric acid chars and burns with agony; carbolic acid whitens and numbs — then quietly depresses the brain and turns the urine smoky green.",["Sulphuric acid: H₂SO₄, colourless, oily, odourless, heavy, hygroscopic; fatal dose 10–20 ml; fatal period 18–24 h; mechanism — dehydration, coagulation, charring; features — immediate burning pain from mouth to stomach, dysphagia, vomiting (black/coffee-ground, acid, with mucosal shreds), excessive salivation, lips/mouth white then black, teeth chalky white, intense thirst, perforation (peritonitis), shock, laryngeal oedema (stridor, asphyxia), aspiration; late — oesophageal and pyloric stricture (gastric outlet obstruction), malnutrition; acid stains on clothes — black/brown holes; nitric acid — yellow (xanthoproteic reaction); HCl — greyish.","Treatment (acids): no emetics, no lavage (perforation), no neutralisation with bicarbonate (CO₂, heat); dilution with small amounts of water/milk early; demulcents; analgesics (morphine), IV fluids, airway management (tracheostomy), PPIs, early endoscopy (within 24–48 h) for grading (Zargar), surgery for perforation, steroids (controversial), dilatation for strictures; skin/eye — copious irrigation.","Vitriolage (acid throwing): face disfigurement, blindness, contractures; S. 124 BNS (326A IPC) — ≥ 10 years to life; free treatment (S. 397 BNSS); Supreme Court (Laxmi v. UoI 2013) — regulation of acid sale; victim compensation ≥ ₹3 lakh; disability (RPwD Act 2016).","Carbolic acid (phenol; C₆H₅OH): colourless needle-shaped crystals turning pink/brown, characteristic smell; fatal dose 10–15 ml/1–2 g; fatal period 3–4 h; mechanism — protoplasmic poison (denatures proteins), local corrosive and anaesthetic, CNS depressant (stimulation then depression), cardiac depression, renal damage; features — burning followed by numbness, white corrugated patches on mouth/lips turning brown, phenolic odour, vomiting rare (anaesthetic), giddiness, unconsciousness, contracted pupils, hypothermia, stertorous breathing, hypotension, convulsions; carboluria — urine olive green, smoky, darkening on exposure (oxidation of hydroquinone, pyrocatechol); ochronosis (chronic).","Treatment (phenol): lavage permitted — warm water with olive oil/castor oil/glycerine or activated charcoal (phenol hardens mucosa; oils dissolve and delay absorption); magnesium sulphate in stomach; skin — wash with polyethylene glycol (PEG 300/400) or glycerine; supportive, sodium bicarbonate for acidosis, haemodialysis; PM — white/grey leathery corroded mucosa, phenol smell, carboluria; preserve viscera in saturated salt (not spirit)."],"H₂SO₄ — black charring; HNO₃ — yellow; carbolic — white→brown, carboluria, lavage allowed."],
"Corrosives are substances that cause direct chemical destruction (corrosion) of tissues at the site of contact; they include strong mineral acids (sulphuric, nitric, hydrochloric), organic acids (carbolic, oxalic, acetic, formic), alkalis (sodium, potassium, ammonium hydroxide, carbonates) and some metallic salts; acids cause coagulative necrosis (eschar limits penetration, so more gastric than oesophageal injury), alkalis cause liquefactive necrosis (saponification, deep penetration — more oesophageal injury). Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄, oil of vitriol, 'gandhak ka tezab'): physical properties — heavy, oily, colourless (commercial — brownish), odourless, non-fuming liquid; highly hygroscopic (absorbs water with generation of heat — exothermic); used in batteries, industries, gold/silver cleaning, toilet cleaners, laboratories; mechanism — extracts water from tissues (dehydration), coagulates proteins (coagulative necrosis), produces heat, converts haemoglobin to acid haematin (black) — tissues become charred/blackened (carbonisation); fatal dose — 10–20 ml of concentrated acid (as little as 3.5–5 ml reported); fatal period — 18–24 hours (immediate deaths from shock/laryngeal oedema; delayed — perforation, peritonitis, mediastinitis, sepsis, strictures — weeks to months). Clinical features (ingestion): immediate intense burning pain extending from mouth to stomach, dysphagia (painful swallowing), drooling, retrosternal and epigastric pain; vomiting — of dark brown/black ('coffee-ground') material containing altered blood, acid and shreds of mucosa (vomit effervesces when it falls on a stone/floor — contains acid — and stains clothing); mouth, lips, tongue, palate — initially greyish-white, then brownish-black and charred; teeth — chalky white (enamel dissolved); excoriation of angles of mouth and chin with brown streaks (dribbling); intense thirst but swallowing difficult; hoarseness, stridor, dyspnoea (laryngeal and glottic oedema — aspiration — may cause asphyxial death); abdominal tenderness and rigidity (perforation of stomach — peritonitis; oesophageal perforation — mediastinitis, pleural effusion); scanty urine (oliguria) with albumin; shock (circulatory collapse — cold clammy skin, rapid feeble pulse), metabolic acidosis, haemolysis, DIC, renal failure; consciousness preserved till near death; complications in survivors — oesophageal stricture (2–8 weeks — dysphagia), pyloric stenosis/antral stricture (gastric outlet obstruction — classically with acids), gastric contraction, malnutrition, carcinoma of oesophagus (long term); skin contact — painful brown-black burns (coagulative), deep ulcers, scarring, contractures; eye — corneal opacification, perforation, blindness; inhalation of fumes — laryngitis, bronchitis, pulmonary oedema. Diagnosis: history, characteristic lesions, pH of vomit, chest/abdominal X-ray (free air), upper GI endoscopy (within 12–48 h; Zargar grading — 0 normal, 1 oedema/hyperaemia, 2a superficial ulcers/exudates, 2b deep/circumferential ulcers, 3a focal necrosis, 3b extensive necrosis), CT (transmural necrosis). Treatment: (1) contraindicated — emetics (re-exposure of oesophagus, perforation), gastric lavage with a stomach tube (perforation risk), neutralisation with bicarbonate/chalk (exothermic heat, CO₂ gas distension — perforation), activated charcoal (no adsorption; obscures endoscopy); (2) dilution — immediately with small amounts of water or milk (within minutes; avoid in airway compromise or perforation) — demulcents (milk, egg white, starch) for soothing; (3) airway — assess for stridor; early endotracheal intubation or tracheostomy in laryngeal oedema; (4) shock — IV fluids, blood transfusion; analgesia (morphine); keep nil by mouth; parenteral nutrition or feeding jejunostomy; PPIs/H₂ blockers; antibiotics if perforation; corticosteroids — controversial (may reduce stricture in grade 2b alkali burns); (5) surgery — emergency laparotomy for perforation, gangrene (oesophagogastrectomy); (6) late — endoscopic dilatation of strictures, oesophageal replacement (colon interposition), gastrojejunostomy; (7) skin and eyes — copious irrigation with water for at least 15–30 minutes, remove contaminated clothing; ophthalmology referral. Post-mortem findings: externally — corrosion and charring (black, brown) of lips, angles of mouth, chin, neck, hands (trickling marks), stained clothes with charred holes; mouth, pharynx, oesophagus — blackened, charred, desquamated mucosa; stomach — black, charred, softened and friable, perforation (stomach contents in peritoneal cavity with blackened peritoneal surfaces and surrounding organs charred), mucosa thrown into folds, coffee-ground contents; acid haematin; larynx — oedema; lungs — aspiration, pulmonary oedema; kidneys — tubular damage; preserve — stomach and contents, intestines, liver, kidney, vomit, stained clothes; preservative — rectified spirit (salt can be used); tests — pH, barium chloride test (white precipitate of barium sulphate). Medicolegal aspects: (1) vitriolage (acid throwing/acid attack) — throwing of corrosive acid (usually sulphuric or nitric, sometimes hydrochloric) on the face and body of the victim (often women — rejected suitors, domestic disputes, revenge, property disputes) to disfigure, blind or kill; injuries — burns of face, scalp, eyes (blindness), neck, chest, arms; deep ulcers, keloids, contractures (ectropion, microstomia), disfigurement, permanent disability, psychological trauma; acid trickle marks (downward flow pattern); legal — S. 124(1) BNS (326A IPC) — voluntarily causing grievous hurt by use of acid etc. — imprisonment not less than 10 years extendable to life and fine (to be paid to the victim, reasonable for medical expenses); S. 124(2) (326B) — attempt — 5–7 years; the 2013 amendment introduced these sections; Laxmi v. Union of India (Supreme Court, 2013, 2015) — regulation of over-the-counter sale of acids (sale only to persons with photo ID, record of buyer, sale to persons above 18), compensation of at least ₹3 lakh to victims (₹1 lakh within 15 days), free medical treatment including reconstructive surgery in all hospitals (public and private) — BNSS S. 397 (CrPC 357C), acid attack victims included as 'disabled' under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016; Poisons (Possession and Sale) Rules; (2) suicidal ingestion (common — toilet cleaners, battery acid); (3) accidental (children, mistaking for water/drink, industrial accidents); (4) homicidal ingestion — rare (taste, pain), but acid may be poured into the mouth of unconscious persons/infants; (5) to destroy dead bodies (dissolve). Nitric acid (aqua fortis): fuming, yellow; stains tissues yellow (xanthoproteic reaction) — yellow lips, teeth, mucosa; effervescent; fatal dose 10–15 ml; used in vitriolage. Hydrochloric acid (muriatic acid, spirit of salt): fuming, irritating; greyish/brown staining; less corrosive. Carbolic acid (phenol, hydroxybenzene, C₆H₅OH): pure phenol — colourless, needle-shaped (prismatic) crystals which turn pink to brown on exposure to air and light; hygroscopic, deliquesce into liquid; characteristic 'phenolic'/carbolic odour; sweetish burning taste; used as disinfectant, antiseptic (Lister), in household cleaners (cresol — Lysol — 50% cresol in soap solution), creosote, dyes, plastics; absorbed through all routes including intact skin; mechanism — (1) protoplasmic (general) poison — denatures and precipitates proteins, (2) local corrosive (coagulates proteins — white eschar) and escharotic with local anaesthetic effect (destroys sensory nerve endings), (3) CNS — initial stimulation then depression (respiratory and vasomotor centre), (4) cardiotoxic (arrhythmias), (5) renal (nephritis), (6) hepatotoxic, (7) haemolysis, methaemoglobinaemia; metabolised to hydroquinone and pyrocatechol (catechol) and conjugated (sulphate/glucuronide), excreted in urine; fatal dose — 10–15 ml of pure liquid (1–2 g; as little as 1 g), Lysol 60–120 ml; fatal period — 3–4 hours (may be within minutes). Clinical features: (1) local — burning pain in mouth, throat and stomach soon followed by tingling and numbness/anaesthesia (so pain is less than with mineral acids); lips, mouth and tongue show white, soft, corrugated (wrinkled) patches of corrosion which later turn brown/grey (hardened, leathery); excoriated skin stains around mouth/chin (white to brown); vomiting rare (anaesthetic and antiemetic action), if present — frothy, phenol-smelling; smell of phenol in breath; (2) systemic (rapid) — giddiness, headache, confusion, staggering, unconsciousness within minutes, pupils contracted (pinpoint), face pale/cyanosed, cold clammy skin, hypothermia, stertorous breathing, feeble rapid pulse, hypotension, shock, convulsions (rare), coma, death from respiratory failure (central) or cardiac arrhythmias; (3) urine — scanty, with albumin; carboluria — urine passed is normal coloured or olive green and darkens (green to black) on standing/exposure to air (due to oxidation of hydroquinone and pyrocatechol to coloured compounds), smoky; (4) renal failure, hepatic damage, haemolysis, metabolic acidosis; (5) chronic exposure — ochronosis (deposition of dark pigment in cartilages — ears, sclera — 'carbolic ochronosis'), phenol marasmus (anorexia, weight loss, nephritis); skin — white then brown corrosive burns with anaesthesia, gangrene of fingers ('carbolic gangrene'), systemic toxicity through skin. Treatment: (1) gastric lavage is permitted (exception to the rule that lavage is contraindicated in corrosive poisoning) because phenol hardens and anaesthetises the gastric mucosa (making perforation unlikely) — lavage with lukewarm water containing olive oil, castor oil, vegetable oil or 10% glycerine (dissolve phenol and delay its absorption — some textbooks warn that oils increase absorption, and activated charcoal or simple water lavage is preferred), or with activated charcoal slurry; at the end, magnesium sulphate (30 g) or sodium sulphate solution left in the stomach (forms harmless sulphocarbolates); (2) skin decontamination — remove clothes; wash with polyethylene glycol (PEG 300 or 400) or glycerine, or isopropyl alcohol, followed by copious water (water alone may spread it); (3) supportive — airway, oxygen, IV fluids, correction of metabolic acidosis (sodium bicarbonate), treatment of shock, arrhythmias (lignocaine), convulsions (diazepam), methaemoglobinaemia (methylene blue), alkaline diuresis, haemodialysis/haemoperfusion in severe cases; (4) demulcents. Post-mortem findings: external — white or brown corroded leathery patches on lips, angles of mouth, chin; phenol odour from body cavities; internal — mucosa of mouth, pharynx, oesophagus and stomach white/grey or brownish, opaque, hard, leathery, thickened, corrugated (thrown into folds), with patches of submucous haemorrhage; perforation rare; congestion of lungs (oedema), brain, kidneys (nephritis); urine — olive green, dark; preserve — stomach and contents, intestine, liver, kidney, blood, urine; preservative — saturated salt solution (not rectified spirit — phenol dissolves in alcohol); test — ferric chloride test (violet/purple colour), bromine water (white precipitate of tribromophenol). Medicolegal: suicidal (formerly common — easily available disinfectant), accidental (mistaken for medicine/drink, applied as antiseptic in excess, children), homicidal rare (smell, taste); used as abortifacient (douche); industrial exposure.",
[["Sulphuric acid vs carbolic acid",[["Feature","Sulphuric acid","Carbolic acid (phenol)"],["Nature","Mineral acid; oily, odourless","Organic; crystals/liquid; phenolic odour"],["Mechanism","Dehydration, coagulation, charring","Protoplasmic poison; local anaesthetic; CNS depressant"],["Mucosa","Black, charred","White corrugated → brown leathery"],["Pain","Intense","Burning then numbness"],["Vomiting","Black, coffee-ground","Rare"],["Systemic","Shock, perforation, laryngeal oedema","Coma, hypothermia, miosis, respiratory failure"],["Urine","Scanty, albuminous","Carboluria (olive green, darkens)"],["Fatal dose","10–20 ml","10–15 ml (1–2 g)"],["Fatal period","18–24 h","3–4 h"],["Gastric lavage","Contraindicated","Permitted (olive oil/glycerine/charcoal)"],["Preservative","Rectified spirit/salt","Saturated salt (not spirit)"]]],
["Management of acid ingestion",["No emetics, no lavage, no neutralisation, no charcoal","Early dilution with small water/milk","Airway (stridor — intubation/tracheostomy)","IV fluids, analgesia, PPIs, NPO","Endoscopy in 12–48 h (Zargar grading)","Surgery for perforation; later dilatation of strictures"]],
["Stains by acid",[["Acid","Colour of stains"],["Sulphuric","Black/brown (charring)"],["Nitric","Yellow (xanthoproteic)"],["Hydrochloric","Greyish"],["Carbolic","White → brown"]]],
["Vitriolage",["Acid thrown on face/body","Disfigurement, blindness, contractures","S. 124 BNS — ≥ 10 yr to life + fine to victim","Laxmi v. UoI (2013): sale regulation, ₹3 lakh compensation, free treatment","RPwD Act 2016 — acid attack survivors"]]],
{c:"Sulphuric acid chars and dehydrates tissues, causing agonising pain, black coffee-ground vomit, laryngeal oedema, perforation and later strictures, and is the classic agent of vitriolage punished under S. 124 BNS, while carbolic acid is a protoplasmic poison that whitens and anaesthetises the mucosa, rapidly produces coma, hypothermia and carboluria, and uniquely among corrosives may be treated by gastric lavage with oil, glycerine or charcoal."});

Z("fmt9",8,"f",["Opium (dried latex of unripe capsules of Papaver somniferum) contains morphine, codeine, thebaine, papaverine and noscapine; opioid poisoning causes the classic triad of pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma, treated with naloxone; cannabis (Cannabis sativa — bhang, ganja, charas; active Δ⁹-THC) is a deliriant/euphoriant producing euphoria, altered time perception, conjunctival injection, tachycardia and, in excess, psychosis ('hashish insanity', run amok); cocaine (from Erythroxylon coca leaves) is a CNS stimulant and local anaesthetic causing euphoria, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, mydriasis, seizures, arrhythmias and formication (cocaine bugs/Magnan's symptom).","Opium sends the brain to sleep and stops breathing; cannabis distorts time and mood; cocaine floods the brain with stimulation, burning the heart.",["Opium: fatal dose 2 g opium / 200 mg morphine (non-addict); fatal period 6–12 h; stages — excitement (brief), stupor, narcosis; features — drowsiness, pinpoint pupils (constricted, react to light), slow shallow respiration (Cheyne–Stokes), cyanosis, bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, coma; differentials — pontine haemorrhage, OP poisoning.","Opioid treatment: airway, ventilation, naloxone 0.4–2 mg IV repeated (up to 10 mg), infusion (short half-life); lavage with KMnO₄ (oxidises morphine) — even late (pylorospasm); body packers — whole bowel irrigation/surgery; PM — froth at mouth, congested oedematous lungs, cyanosis, needle marks (addicts), opium smell in stomach; chronic — morphinism (constipation, miosis, emaciation, needle tracks, abscesses, withdrawal — yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, piloerection, mydriasis, diarrhoea).","Cannabis: preparations — bhang (dried leaves/stalks — least potent; drunk as beverage), ganja (flowering tops of female plant — smoked), charas/hashish (resin — most potent); acute — euphoria, laughter, talkativeness, distorted time/space perception, hallucinations, increased appetite, dry mouth, red eyes (conjunctival injection), tachycardia, ataxia, then drowsiness; large doses — panic, acute psychosis; run amok (homicidal frenzy); chronic — amotivational syndrome, cannabis psychosis, bronchitis; treatment — reassurance, benzodiazepines; Duquenois–Levine test.","Cocaine: 'coke', crack (free base — smoked); fatal dose 1–1.2 g (oral), less IV; mechanism — blocks reuptake of dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin; Na⁺ channel block (local anaesthetic); acute — euphoria, excitement, hallucinations, mydriasis, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, seizures, MI, stroke, aortic dissection, arrhythmias, rhabdomyolysis, excited delirium; chronic — perforation of nasal septum (snorting), formication (Magnan's symptom — 'cocaine bugs' under skin), paranoia, weight loss; treatment — benzodiazepines, cooling, avoid β-blockers (unopposed α), nitrates, phentolamine.","Medicolegal (all under NDPS Act 1985): opium — suicidal, accidental (children given opium 'afeem' to sleep — infanticide), homicidal (rare), addiction, overdose deaths, body packers; cannabis — crimes under intoxication, impaired driving, run amok; cocaine — sudden deaths, excited delirium in custody, body packers/stuffers; tolerance and dependence; criminal responsibility — voluntary intoxication no defence."],"Opium: miosis, respiratory depression, coma — naloxone; cannabis: euphoria, red eyes, psychosis; cocaine: stimulant, formication, septal perforation."],
"Opium ('afeem'): the air-dried milky latex obtained by incising the unripe seed capsules (pods) of the poppy plant Papaver somniferum (cultivated legally in India under licence — Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, UP — for medicinal purposes); dark brown, sticky, with a characteristic odour and bitter taste; contains about 25 alkaloids — phenanthrene group (morphine 9–17% — principal; codeine 0.5%; thebaine — convulsant) and benzylisoquinoline group (papaverine — smooth muscle relaxant; noscapine/narcotine — antitussive); derivatives — heroin (diacetylmorphine — 'smack', 'brown sugar' in India — 2–3 times more potent, lipid-soluble), synthetic and semisynthetic opioids (pethidine, methadone, fentanyl — extremely potent, tramadol, buprenorphine, pentazocine, dextropropoxyphene, codeine cough syrups — 'phensedyl abuse'); poppy straw/husk ('doda post' — consumed in Punjab/Rajasthan); mechanism — agonists at μ (mu), κ and δ opioid receptors in CNS and gut → analgesia, euphoria, sedation, respiratory depression (reduced sensitivity of medullary centre to CO₂), miosis (stimulation of Edinger–Westphal nucleus), cough suppression, nausea, constipation, histamine release; fatal dose — opium ~ 2 g, morphine ~ 200 mg (non-tolerant; tolerant addicts may take grams); fatal period — 6–12 hours. Clinical features of acute opium poisoning: three stages — (1) stage of excitement (short or absent) — feeling of well-being, euphoria, talkativeness, flushed face, restlessness; (2) stage of stupor — headache, giddiness, drowsiness, nausea/vomiting, irresistible desire to sleep, pinpoint pupils, face cyanosed, patient can be roused with difficulty; (3) stage of narcosis/coma — deep coma, cannot be roused, muscles flaccid, reflexes absent, pupils pinpoint (react to light; dilate terminally from asphyxia), respiration slow (2–4/min), shallow, stertorous, Cheyne–Stokes, cyanosis, bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, skin cold and clammy, non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (froth at mouth), convulsions (children, pethidine, tramadol), death from respiratory failure; classic triad — pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression + coma; differential diagnosis of pinpoint pupils with coma — pontine haemorrhage (hyperpyrexia), OP/carbamate poisoning (secretions, fasciculations), clonidine, phenothiazines; diagnosis — therapeutic response to naloxone, urine drug screen (immunoassay for opiates, GC-MS confirmation), blood levels. Treatment: (1) airway, oxygen, assisted ventilation (bag-mask, intubation); (2) naloxone (competitive opioid antagonist) — 0.4–2 mg IV (or IM/SC/intranasal), repeated every 2–3 minutes up to 10 mg; failure to respond suggests another cause; because naloxone's duration (30–90 min) is shorter than most opioids, the patient may relapse into coma ('renarcotisation') — continuous infusion (two-thirds of the effective bolus dose per hour) and observation for at least 6–24 h (methadone, sustained-release); in opioid-dependent persons, cautious small doses (0.04–0.1 mg) to avoid precipitating acute withdrawal; (3) gastric lavage with potassium permanganate (1:5000 — oxidises morphine) or activated charcoal — useful even several hours after oral ingestion (opioids cause pylorospasm and delayed gastric emptying; morphine is re-excreted into stomach); (4) body packers (swallowed packets) — whole bowel irrigation, surgery for obstruction/rupture; (5) supportive — warmth, fluids, management of pulmonary oedema, catheterisation (urinary retention), treat seizures; avoid stimulants. Chronic opium poisoning (opiate addiction, morphinism): tolerance and physical dependence; constipation, anorexia, emaciation, pinpoint pupils, sallow skin, dirty appearance, needle tracks ('tracks') along veins, abscesses, thrombophlebitis, tattoos over veins, impotence, amenorrhoea, infections (HIV, HBV, HCV, endocarditis — tricuspid), moral deterioration, crime; withdrawal syndrome (begins 6–12 h after last dose of heroin/morphine; peak 36–72 h) — craving, anxiety, yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, sweating, piloerection ('cold turkey'), mydriasis, abdominal cramps, vomiting, diarrhoea, muscle aches ('kicking the habit'), tachycardia, hypertension, insomnia — not life-threatening in adults (neonatal withdrawal can be); treatment — opioid substitution (methadone, buprenorphine), clonidine, naltrexone for relapse prevention, rehabilitation. Post-mortem findings (opioid poisoning): external — cyanosis, froth at mouth and nostrils (white/blood-tinged — pulmonary oedema), pinpoint pupils (not reliable after death), needle marks (fresh and old) in addicts, signs of chronic IV use; internal — signs of asphyxia, heavy congested oedematous lungs (> 1 kg each), froth in airways, congested brain and viscera, distended bladder, smell of opium in stomach contents (with opium particles); endocarditis, granulomas (talc) in lungs of chronic IV users; preserve stomach and contents, liver, kidney, blood, urine (most useful), bile (morphine concentrates), vitreous, injection site skin; hair (chronic use). Cannabis (Indian hemp, marijuana, Cannabis sativa/indica): a herbaceous plant; active principles — cannabinoids, especially Δ⁹-tetrahydrocannabinol (Δ⁹-THC — psychoactive), cannabidiol (CBD — non-psychoactive), cannabinol; preparations in India — (1) bhang (siddhi, sabzi) — dried leaves and stalks (fruiting shoots) of both male and female plants; least potent (THC ~ 1%); consumed as a drink (with milk, sugar, spices — thandai, especially during Holi and Shivratri), or as sweets (bhang ki goli, laddoo); (2) ganja — dried flowering tops (with resin) of unfertilised female plants; more potent (THC ~ 5–15%); smoked in pipes (chillum) or cigarettes ('joint'); (3) charas (hashish) — resin exuded from leaves and flowering tops of the plant; most potent (THC 15–40%); smoked; (4) hash oil; synthetic cannabinoids ('spice', 'K2'); legal status (NDPS Act 1985) — ganja and charas are narcotic drugs (cultivation, possession, sale prohibited), but bhang (leaves and seeds) is excluded from the definition of 'cannabis' under the NDPS Act and is regulated by state excise laws (legal sale in licensed shops in some states); mechanism — THC acts on cannabinoid CB1 receptors (brain — limbic system, hippocampus, cerebellum, basal ganglia) and CB2 (immune); effects onset — smoking within minutes (peak 30 min, lasts 2–4 h); oral 30 min–2 h (lasts 4–12 h); fatal dose — not established (deaths from cannabis alone are extremely rare — practically non-fatal); clinical features of acute intoxication: (1) stage of excitement — euphoria (feeling 'high'), sense of well-being, relaxation, uncontrollable laughter, talkativeness, heightened sensory perception (colours, music), distortion of time (time seems prolonged) and space (distances appear increased), depersonalisation, fragmented thoughts, impaired short-term memory, attention and judgement, impaired motor coordination (ataxia), visual and auditory hallucinations (illusions), increased appetite ('munchies'), dry mouth, injected conjunctivae ('red eyes' — characteristic), tachycardia, postural hypotension, normal or slightly dilated pupils; aphrodisiac belief; (2) stage of narcosis — drowsiness, sleep; with large doses — anxiety, panic attacks, paranoia, acute toxic psychosis (delusions, hallucinations, agitation), confusion, delirium; in predisposed — precipitates schizophrenia; 'run amok' — a state of homicidal frenzy (a Malayan term) in which the person, under the influence of cannabis (or other causes), indiscriminately attacks and kills anybody coming in his way, often ending in suicide or being killed — historically associated with charas use; children — accidental ingestion (edibles) — drowsiness, ataxia, hypotonia, coma; chronic use (cannabis use disorder) — amotivational syndrome (apathy, lack of ambition, poor concentration), cognitive impairment, chronic bronchitis, cannabis psychosis ('hashish insanity' — delusions, hallucinations, violence), cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome (cyclic vomiting relieved by hot showers), gynaecomastia, reduced fertility, tolerance and mild withdrawal (irritability, insomnia); diagnosis — urine immunoassay (THC-COOH metabolite — detectable for days to weeks in chronic users), GC-MS; Duquenois–Levine test (colour test for cannabis — violet in chloroform layer), microscopic identification of plant (cystolithic hairs — bear-claw shaped); treatment — reassurance in a quiet room, benzodiazepines for anxiety/agitation, antipsychotics for psychosis, supportive; PM — non-specific (congestion); medicolegal — crimes under intoxication (violence, sexual offences), impaired driving (RTAs), run amok, use to stupefy victims (with datura), possession offences under NDPS Act. Cocaine: an alkaloid (benzoylmethylecgonine) obtained from the leaves of Erythroxylon coca (South America — Bolivia, Peru, Colombia); forms — cocaine hydrochloride (white crystalline powder — 'coke', 'snow', 'C', 'charlie' — snorted, injected, rubbed on gums), free base/crack cocaine (smoked — rapid intense effect, highly addictive), coca paste; in India, cocaine abuse occurs in metros (party drug) and smuggling cases; mechanism — (1) blocks reuptake of dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin at presynaptic terminals (monoamine transporters) → intense sympathetic stimulation and dopaminergic euphoria (reward pathway — nucleus accumbens) → addiction; (2) blocks fast sodium channels (local anaesthetic and class I antiarrhythmic effect — QRS widening); (3) vasoconstriction; cocaethylene (formed with alcohol) — more toxic; fatal dose — about 1–1.2 g orally (much less IV or smoked; as little as 20 mg in sensitive individuals); fatal period — minutes to hours; clinical features of acute poisoning: (1) CNS stimulation — euphoria, excitement, talkativeness, increased energy, alertness, grandiosity, reduced fatigue and hunger, restlessness, anxiety, agitation, paranoia, hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile — formication), stereotyped behaviour, tremors, hyperreflexia, convulsions, coma; excited (agitated) delirium — extreme agitation, violence, hyperthermia, superhuman strength, sudden cardiac arrest (especially during restraint in police custody); (2) sympathomimetic — mydriasis (dilated pupils), tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia (malignant), sweating, tachypnoea; (3) cardiovascular — chest pain, myocardial ischaemia and infarction (coronary vasospasm, thrombosis — in young persons), arrhythmias (ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation, QRS widening), aortic dissection, cardiomyopathy, myocarditis; (4) cerebrovascular — stroke (haemorrhagic, ischaemic), subarachnoid haemorrhage; (5) others — rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury, intestinal ischaemia, pulmonary oedema, 'crack lung', pneumothorax (smoking), DIC, placental abruption and fetal harm (pregnancy); death from arrhythmia, hyperthermia, seizures, stroke, respiratory failure; chronic use (cocainism) — perforation of the nasal septum (ischaemic necrosis due to vasoconstriction from snorting), chronic rhinitis, loss of smell, weight loss, insomnia, paranoid psychosis, formication — tactile hallucination of insects crawling under the skin (Magnan's symptom — 'cocaine bugs'), leading to excoriations, scratch marks; dilated cardiomyopathy, accelerated atherosclerosis; tolerance (to euphoria), dependence (intense craving), withdrawal ('crash' — dysphoria, fatigue, depression, hypersomnia, suicidal ideation); diagnosis — urine benzoylecgonine (immunoassay; detectable 2–4 days), blood cocaine; treatment — benzodiazepines (first-line for agitation, seizures, hypertension, tachycardia), aggressive cooling for hyperthermia, IV fluids (rhabdomyolysis), nitroglycerin and phentolamine or calcium channel blockers for hypertension/chest pain, sodium bicarbonate for QRS widening, aspirin for ACS; avoid β-blockers (unopposed α-adrenergic vasoconstriction — worsens hypertension and coronary spasm); antipsychotics cautiously; body packers (swallowing packets for smuggling) and body stuffers (swallowing to avoid arrest) — risk of rupture and massive fatal toxicity — whole bowel irrigation, surgical removal; PM — non-specific congestion, pulmonary oedema, cerebral haemorrhage, myocardial contraction band necrosis, nasal septum perforation, needle marks, packets in GIT; preserve blood with sodium fluoride (inhibits cholinesterase-mediated hydrolysis), urine, brain, nasal swabs. Medicolegal (common to narcotic drugs): Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985 (NDPS — amended 1988, 2001, 2014, 2021): prohibits production, manufacture, possession, sale, purchase, transport, import, export, use and consumption of narcotic drugs (opium, morphine, heroin, cocaine, coca leaf, cannabis — ganja, charas) and psychotropic substances, except for medical and scientific purposes; punishment graded by quantity — small quantity (up to 1 year/fine ₹10,000), more than small but less than commercial (up to 10 years/₹1 lakh), commercial quantity (10–20 years rigorous imprisonment and fine ₹1–2 lakh); repeat offences — up to death penalty (S. 31A — for commercial quantities, now discretionary); consumption (S. 27) — up to 1 year (cocaine, morphine, heroin) or 6 months (others); immunity for addicts volunteering for treatment (S. 64A); 2014 amendment — 'essential narcotic drugs' (morphine, fentanyl, methadone) for pain relief made easier to access (palliative care); NCB (Narcotics Control Bureau) enforces; medicolegal issues — overdose deaths (suicidal, accidental — heroin purity, fentanyl contamination), homicidal (rare — opium in food; opium given to infants to quieten them — accidental/infanticide), criminal acts under intoxication, impaired driving (MV Act S. 185 — drugs), sudden deaths in custody (excited delirium, body packers), drug-facilitated crimes, examination of persons for drug intoxication (with consent/under BNSS S. 51), testing of seized samples by FSL, compulsory treatment; criminal responsibility — voluntary intoxication is no defence (S. 23 BNS); involuntary intoxication (administered without knowledge or against will) is a defence under S. 23 BNS.",
[["Opium, cannabis, cocaine — comparison",[["Feature","Opium/morphine","Cannabis","Cocaine"],["Source","Papaver somniferum latex","Cannabis sativa (bhang, ganja, charas)","Erythroxylon coca leaves"],["Active principle","Morphine, codeine","Δ⁹-THC","Benzoylmethylecgonine"],["Class","CNS depressant (narcotic)","Deliriant/euphoriant","CNS stimulant, local anaesthetic"],["Pupils","Pinpoint","Normal/slightly dilated; red eyes","Dilated"],["Key acute features","Coma, respiratory depression, hypothermia","Euphoria, time distortion, hallucinations, tachycardia","Euphoria, agitation, hypertension, hyperthermia, seizures, MI"],["Chronic","Constipation, needle tracks, withdrawal","Amotivational syndrome, psychosis","Septal perforation, formication (Magnan's)"],["Fatal dose","Opium 2 g; morphine 200 mg","Practically non-fatal","~1–1.2 g oral"],["Treatment","Naloxone, ventilation, KMnO₄ lavage","Reassurance, benzodiazepines","Benzodiazepines, cooling; avoid β-blockers"],["Special","Infants given opium","Run amok","Excited delirium; body packers"]]],
["Cannabis preparations",[["Preparation","Part","Potency","Legal (NDPS)"],["Bhang","Dried leaves, stalks","Least","Excluded (state excise)"],["Ganja","Flowering tops of female plant","Moderate","Prohibited"],["Charas/hashish","Resin","Most","Prohibited"]]],
["Opioid withdrawal",["Yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea","Piloerection, sweating, mydriasis","Abdominal cramps, vomiting, diarrhoea","Myalgia, insomnia, craving","Treatment: methadone/buprenorphine, clonidine"]],
["NDPS Act 1985",["Covers opium, morphine, heroin, cocaine, ganja, charas, psychotropics","Graded punishment by quantity (small/intermediate/commercial)","Consumption — S. 27","Immunity for addicts seeking treatment — S. 64A","2014: access to essential narcotic drugs for pain relief"]]],
{c:"Opium and its alkaloids cause the triad of pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma reversed by naloxone, cannabis preparations from bhang to charas produce euphoria, time distortion, red eyes and sometimes psychosis or running amok, and cocaine causes sympathomimetic excitation with seizures, hyperthermia and cardiovascular catastrophes plus septal perforation and formication with chronic use; all are regulated under the NDPS Act."});

Z("fmt9",9,"f",["Abrus precatorius (rati, gunchi, jequirity, rosary pea) seeds — bright scarlet with a black spot at the hilum (white variety also) — contain abrin, a toxalbumin (ribosome-inactivating protein like ricin) which inhibits protein synthesis; intact seeds pass harmlessly but crushed seeds or extracts are toxic, and the classic criminal use in India is the 'sui' (needle) — a pointed spike made from paste of crushed seeds, dried, and thrust into cattle (cattle poisoning for hides) or rarely humans, causing local necrosis and systemic toxicity resembling viper bite; other plant poisons include castor, croton, calotropis, semecarpus, oleander, aconite, nux vomica, and ergot.","Rati seeds are pretty but deadly when broken — their protein toxin shuts down the cell's protein factory; criminals shape them into tiny poisoned needles (sui).",["Seeds: ovoid, bright red (scarlet) with black spot at one end (hilum), glossy, hard coat; ~ 1/8 g each (used as jewellers' weight — 'rati' ~ 120 mg); white and black varieties; fatal dose 1–2 well-chewed seeds; fatal period 3–5 days.","Abrin: toxalbumin (two chains — B binds galactose receptors, A inhibits 60S ribosome → cell death); also haemagglutinin; ingestion of crushed seeds — delayed (hours) severe gastroenteritis, haemorrhage, dehydration, hepatic and renal failure, convulsions; ocular — conjunctivitis (jequirity ophthalmia).","Sui (needle) poisoning: seeds soaked, crushed, mixed with dhatura/opium/arsenic, moulded into conical spikes (needles ~ 2 cm), dried in sun, mounted on handle, thrust into skin of cattle (or humans) — local pain, swelling, oedema, haemorrhagic necrosis, cellulitis, then drowsiness, collapse, convulsions, death in 1–3 days (resembles viper bite — but no fang marks; spike remnants in wound).","Treatment: decontamination (lavage, charcoal) for ingestion; excision of wound and removal of sui fragments; supportive — fluids, blood products; anti-abrin serum (historically); no specific antidote; PM — local haemorrhagic necrosis with sui remnant, haemorrhages in viscera; preserve tissue around wound, sui, viscera; tests — precipitin with anti-abrin serum.","Medicolegal: cattle poisoning (Chamars historically — for hides) — Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, S. 325 BNS (mischief by killing animals); rarely homicidal (sui into humans), accidental (children chewing seeds, rosary/jewellery makers pricking fingers), abortifacient (paste on stick), suicidal rare; other plant irritants — castor (ricin), croton, calotropis (madar juice for infanticide, abortion), semecarpus (marking nut — vesicant used to fake injuries), capsicum (chilli in eyes/genitals)."],"Abrus: abrin (ribosome-inactivating); 'sui' cattle poisoning; resembles viper bite without fang marks."],
"Abrus precatorius (Leguminosae/Fabaceae; synonyms — rati, gunchi, gunja, chirmiti, jequirity bean, rosary pea, crab's eye, Indian liquorice): a climbing creeper growing all over India; its seeds are ovoid, about 6–8 mm, with a hard, glossy, smooth coat — the common variety bright scarlet (red) with a black spot (patch) at one end around the hilum; other varieties — white with a black spot, black with white spot; each seed weighs about 1/8 g (~ 105–120 mg) — formerly used as a standard weight by goldsmiths ('rati'), and for making rosaries, necklaces, bangles and toys; the roots and leaves have a sweet taste (liquorice-like; glycyrrhizin) and are used in folk medicine. Toxic principle: abrin (abrin A, B, C, D) — a highly toxic glycoprotein/toxalbumin (phytotoxin) similar to ricin of castor seeds — a type II ribosome-inactivating protein consisting of two polypeptide chains linked by a disulphide bond: the B chain (haptomer — lectin) binds to galactose-containing glycoproteins/glycolipids on cell surfaces and facilitates entry by endocytosis; the A chain (effectomer — N-glycosidase) depurinates a specific adenine residue of the 28S rRNA in the 60S ribosomal subunit, irreversibly inhibiting protein synthesis (elongation factor binding), leading to cell death (one molecule can kill a cell); also contains abric acid, abrine (an amino acid — N-methyltryptophan), haemagglutinins (abrus agglutinin) causing agglutination and haemolysis; abrin is one of the most potent plant toxins (more toxic than ricin) — fatal dose of purified abrin ~ 0.1–1 µg/kg (estimated); seeds — 1–2 well-chewed or crushed seeds may be fatal in a child; the hard seed coat prevents absorption if swallowed whole — intact seeds usually pass unchanged through the gut (non-toxic); abrin is destroyed by heat (boiling) and by gastric juices partially; fatal period — 3–5 days (ingestion); 1–3 days (parenteral/sui). Clinical features: (1) ingestion of crushed/chewed seeds — after a latent period of a few hours to 2–3 days: nausea, vomiting, severe abdominal colic, profuse watery or bloody diarrhoea (haemorrhagic gastroenteritis), dehydration, hypovolaemic shock, drowsiness, weakness, tremors, convulsions; later — hepatic necrosis (jaundice, raised enzymes), renal failure (oliguria, haematuria), haemolysis, retinal haemorrhages, cerebral oedema, cardiac arrhythmias, multi-organ failure, death; (2) parenteral — by injection of extract or by 'sui' (needle) — rapid local and systemic effects: local pain, swelling, oedema, ecchymosis, haemorrhagic necrosis and ulceration at the site; systemic — weakness, drowsiness, prostration, nausea, vomiting, cold clammy skin, rapid feeble pulse, convulsions, cardiac failure, death in 1–3 days — the picture resembles viperine snake bite (local haemorrhagic swelling and systemic collapse) but there are no fang marks; instead a punctured wound with the remains of the needle may be found; (3) eye — instillation of seed extract (jequirity — formerly used to treat trachoma/pannus) causes severe conjunctivitis, chemosis, keratitis, corneal ulceration, panophthalmitis — 'jequirity ophthalmia'; (4) skin contact — dermatitis; occupational — pricking of fingers by rosary/jewellery makers drilling seeds (toxic). Sui (needle) poisoning: the classical criminal use of abrus in India; method of preparation — seeds are soaked in water, crushed/pounded into a paste (sometimes mixed with dhatura, opium, arsenic, onion juice or spirit to enhance effect), rolled into small sharp-pointed conical spikes called 'suis' (needles) about 1.5–2 cm (5/8 inch) long, weighing ~ 90–120 mg, dried in the sun and hardened; when dry they are hard, greyish-brown/black, and resemble a large needle or thorn; they are inserted (fixed) into a wooden handle (or between fingers) and thrust forcibly into the skin of the victim (usually cattle — near the neck, abdomen, hump, flank, anus, vulva) — the handle is withdrawn and the sui breaks off and remains in the tissues; used for cattle poisoning (to obtain hides — historically by certain communities, or for revenge), and occasionally for homicide in humans; the animal shows local swelling, haemorrhagic oedema, and dies in 24–36 hours (cattle); in humans similar course. Diagnosis: history; seeds/fragments in vomitus, stomach contents (identification of seed coat — red with black spot) or sui fragments in the wound; laboratory — precipitin test using anti-abrin serum (immunoassay), ELISA, LC-MS for abrine (urinary marker of abrin exposure); haemagglutination test; animal inoculation (historical). Treatment: no specific antidote; (1) ingestion — gastric lavage (if crushed seeds ingested within hours), activated charcoal, whole bowel irrigation (seeds); (2) parenteral/sui — excision of the wound with removal of the sui fragments and surrounding tissue, local care; (3) supportive — aggressive IV fluids and electrolytes, blood transfusion, management of shock, hepatic and renal failure (haemodialysis), anticonvulsants, monitoring; (4) anti-abrin serum (prepared by immunising animals — historically by Ehrlich; experimental); vaccines and antibodies under research; (5) eye — irrigation, topical steroids/antibiotics. Post-mortem findings: local — at the site of sui puncture: a small punctured wound with surrounding haemorrhagic infiltration, oedema, necrosis, gangrene; sui fragments may be found embedded (should be recovered); ingestion — haemorrhagic inflammation of stomach and intestine, ulceration, seed fragments; systemic — petechial and ecchymotic haemorrhages in serous membranes, heart, lungs, brain; fatty degeneration and necrosis of liver, kidneys; congestion; samples — tissue around the wound (with sui) and control tissue, stomach and contents, intestine, liver, kidney, blood (frozen or in saturated salt — avoid heat). Medicolegal aspects: (1) cattle poisoning — chiefly by sui (also by arsenic, aconite, calotropis, oleander); motive — to obtain hides (historically), revenge on the owner, to collect insurance; offence — S. 325 BNS (428–429 IPC — mischief by killing, poisoning, maiming animals), Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act 1960; veterinary post-mortem; (2) homicidal — rare (sui thrust into humans; extract given in food is unreliable as seeds are not well absorbed); (3) accidental — children chewing attractive seeds (jewellery, toys), finger pricks in rosary makers; (4) suicidal — rare (crushed seeds); (5) criminal abortion — paste of seeds applied on an abortion stick or inserted into the vagina/cervix; also taken orally as an abortifacient (with other plants); (6) as weights by jewellers; (7) bioterrorism concern (abrin, ricin — Schedule 1 toxins of the Chemical Weapons Convention). Other plant irritant poisons (brief): castor (Ricinus communis — ricin; seeds mottled, ingestion of chewed seeds — gastroenteritis, haemorrhage, organ failure; ricin used in assassination — Georgi Markov umbrella murder 1978), croton (Croton tiglium — jamalgota; crotin and croton oil — violent purgative, vesicant), calotropis (madar — milky juice — irritant; used for infanticide, abortion, cattle poisoning), semecarpus (marking nut — bhilawa; black juice — vesicant — used to fabricate injuries simulating bruises, criminal abortion), capsicum (chilli — thrown in eyes during robbery, applied to genitals in torture), ergot (Claviceps purpurea — abortifacient, gangrene); see fmt9 topic 11; cardiac plant poisons — oleander, aconite, nux vomica (strychnine — convulsant); see topic 10.",
[["Abrus precatorius — key facts",[["Feature","Detail"],["Common names","Rati, gunchi, jequirity, rosary pea"],["Seeds","Ovoid, scarlet with black spot at hilum; ~120 mg each"],["Toxin","Abrin — toxalbumin, ribosome-inactivating (A + B chains)"],["Mechanism","Depurinates 28S rRNA → stops protein synthesis"],["Whole seeds","Usually pass intact (hard coat)"],["Fatal dose","1–2 crushed seeds (child); µg/kg purified abrin"],["Fatal period","3–5 days (ingestion); 1–3 days (sui)"],["Antidote","None — supportive; excision of sui"]]],
["Sui (needle) poisoning",[["Aspect","Detail"],["Preparation","Seeds soaked, crushed to paste (± dhatura, opium, arsenic), moulded into spikes ~2 cm, sun-dried"],["Use","Thrust into cattle (hides, revenge); rarely humans"],["Local","Pain, swelling, haemorrhagic necrosis; sui fragment in wound"],["Systemic","Drowsiness, collapse, convulsions, death 1–3 days"],["Resembles","Viper bite — but no fang marks"],["Law","S. 325 BNS (animals), PCA Act; murder if human"]]],
["Clinical features of ingestion",["Latent period hours–days","Haemorrhagic gastroenteritis, dehydration, shock","Hepatic necrosis, renal failure, haemolysis","Convulsions, cerebral oedema","Eye: jequirity ophthalmia"]],
["Other irritant plant poisons",[["Plant","Toxin","Use"],["Castor","Ricin","Accidental; assassination"],["Croton","Crotin, croton oil","Purgative; vesicant"],["Calotropis","Milky latex","Infanticide, abortion, cattle"],["Semecarpus","Black juice (vesicant)","Fabricated injuries, abortion"],["Capsicum","Capsaicin","Robbery (eyes), torture"]]]],
{c:"Abrus precatorius seeds, scarlet with a black hilar spot, contain abrin, a ribosome-inactivating toxalbumin that is harmless in intact seeds but lethal when crushed, causing delayed haemorrhagic gastroenteritis and multi-organ failure; in India it is classically fashioned into dried 'sui' needles thrust into cattle or occasionally people, producing viper-bite-like local necrosis and collapse without fang marks, with no antidote beyond excision and supportive care."});

Z("fmt9",10,"f",["Cardiac poisons act chiefly on the heart: oleanders — yellow oleander (Cerbera thevetia/Thevetia peruviana — thevetin) and white/pink oleander (Nerium odorum — nerin, oleandrin) — contain cardiac glycosides that inhibit Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase like digoxin, causing vomiting, bradycardia, heart blocks, ventricular arrhythmias and hyperkalaemia (treated with atropine, pacing, activated charcoal and digoxin-specific Fab); aconite (Aconitum ferox/napellus — 'mitha zahar', bikh; aconitine) opens voltage-gated sodium channels persistently, producing tingling and numbness of mouth spreading over the body, vomiting, hypotension and fatal ventricular arrhythmias — it is one of the most toxic plants, used in Ayurvedic medicine and historically for homicide.","Oleander acts like a digoxin overdose — slow heart and blocks; aconite jams sodium channels open — tingling numbness and chaotic arrhythmias.",["Yellow oleander (pila kaner): all parts toxic, seeds most (8–10 seeds fatal); glycosides thevetin A, B, neriifolin, peruvoside; features — burning mouth, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, dizziness, bradycardia, AV block (PR prolongation, Wenckebach, complete block), sinus arrest, VT/VF, hyperkalaemia; suicidal poisoning common in Sri Lanka, south India.","White oleander (safed kaner, Nerium indicum): root most toxic, used for abortion (root as abortion stick), suicide, cattle poisoning; glycosides oleandrin, neriodorin, neriodorein (pseudocurarine-like); similar cardiac effects plus muscle twitching, tetanic spasms.","Treatment of oleander: lavage, MDAC (multiple-dose activated charcoal — reduces mortality), atropine for bradycardia, temporary pacing, correct K⁺ (avoid calcium), anti-digoxin Fab (digoxin immune fab — 'DigiFab'), lignocaine/phenytoin for ventricular arrhythmias; serum digoxin assay cross-reacts (qualitative).","Aconite: Aconitum napellus (monkshood), A. ferox (Indian aconite — 'bikh', 'mitha zahar'); tuberous root resembles horseradish (but horseradish is pungent, aconite sweet initially then tingling); alkaloid aconitine (also pseudaconitine); fatal dose 1 g root / 2–5 mg aconitine; fatal period 2–6 h; mechanism — persistent activation of Na⁺ channels → delayed depolarisation; features — tingling and numbness of tongue, mouth, throat spreading to face, limbs ('ants crawling'), salivation, vomiting, abdominal pain, muscle weakness, giddiness, sweating, hypotension, bradycardia/tachyarrhythmias (VT, torsades, bidirectional VT, VF), convulsions; consciousness preserved.","Treatment of aconite: lavage, charcoal, atropine (bradycardia), amiodarone/flecainide/lignocaine, magnesium, cardioversion often ineffective, cardiopulmonary bypass/ECMO; PM non-specific; preserve viscera; medicolegal — homicidal (historic — 'ideal' for killing: small dose, few signs), suicidal, accidental (Ayurvedic preparations improperly processed — 'shodhana' detoxification; mistaken for horseradish), arrow poison, cattle poisoning."],"Oleander = digoxin-like (bradycardia, blocks; Fab); aconite = Na⁺ channel opener (tingling numbness, VT)."],
"Cardiac poisons are substances whose principal toxic action is on the heart; important ones in India include yellow and white oleanders, aconite, digitalis, tobacco (nicotine), cerbera (Cerbera odollam — 'suicide tree' of Kerala), and quinine. (1) Yellow oleander (Thevetia peruviana; Cerbera thevetia; 'pila kaner', 'be-still tree', 'lucky nut'): an ornamental shrub common in gardens and roadsides throughout India; leaves linear, flowers bell-shaped yellow (or orange/white); fruit — triangular, green turning black, contains a hard nut with 2–4 seeds; all parts contain a milky latex and are toxic, especially the seeds (kernels) — 8–10 seeds (or 1–2 in children) can be fatal; toxic principles — cardiac glycosides (cardenolides): thevetin A and B, thevetoxin, peruvoside, neriifolin, ruvoside, cerberin; (2) White/pink oleander (Nerium odorum, Nerium indicum, Nerium oleander; 'safed kaner', 'kaner', 'karabi'): shrub with lance-shaped leaves in whorls of three, pink/white/red fragrant flowers, long slender pods; all parts toxic, especially the root (most toxic) and leaves; toxic principles — oleandrin (most important), neriodorin, neriodorein, folinerin, rosaginin; neriodorein has strychnine-like action on spinal cord (tetanic convulsions) and pseudocurarine-like action on muscle; (3) mechanism (both oleanders, like digoxin): inhibition of membrane Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump in myocardial cells → increased intracellular Na⁺ → reduced Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange → increased intracellular Ca²⁺ (increased contractility, delayed afterdepolarisations — ectopy, arrhythmias); increased vagal tone (bradycardia, AV nodal block); extracellular K⁺ rises (hyperkalaemia — marker of severity); fatal dose — yellow oleander: 8–10 seeds; 15–20 g of root (white oleander); fatal period — 2–3 hours to 24 h (may be up to 36 h); (4) clinical features — local: burning and tingling sensation in mouth, numbness of tongue, salivation (bitter seeds); gastrointestinal: nausea, persistent vomiting (early — central), abdominal pain, diarrhoea; cardiovascular (the main danger): bradycardia (sinus bradycardia), various heart blocks — prolonged PR interval, first-degree, second-degree (Mobitz I — Wenckebach), complete heart block, sino-atrial block, sinus arrest, junctional rhythms, atrial tachycardia with block, ventricular ectopics (bigeminy), ventricular tachycardia (including bidirectional VT), ventricular fibrillation; ECG — ST depression ('reverse tick/sagging ST' — digitalis effect), T-wave inversion; hypotension, shock; CNS: dizziness, weakness, drowsiness, confusion, visual disturbances (xanthopsia — yellow vision, halos), dilated pupils, convulsions (white oleander — tetanic spasms), coma; metabolic: hyperkalaemia (poor prognostic sign); death from cardiac arrhythmia/arrest; (5) diagnosis — history, ECG, serum potassium; serum digoxin immunoassay cross-reacts with oleander glycosides (qualitative positive, not quantitative); identification of seeds/leaves in vomitus; LC-MS for oleandrin/thevetin; (6) treatment — gastric lavage (if early), activated charcoal — single dose followed by multiple-dose activated charcoal (MDAC — 50 g every 6 h for 3 days — interrupts enterohepatic circulation; Sri Lankan trial (de Silva 2003) showed reduced mortality); atropine (0.6–1.2 mg IV, repeated) for bradycardia and AV block; isoprenaline; temporary transvenous cardiac pacing for symptomatic bradyarrhythmias/complete heart block; correction of hyperkalaemia (insulin–dextrose, bicarbonate; avoid calcium — may precipitate arrhythmias ('stone heart')); anti-arrhythmics for ventricular arrhythmias — lignocaine, phenytoin, magnesium sulphate; avoid quinidine, procainamide; cardioversion at low energy (risk of VF); digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab — DigiFab/Digibind) — bind cardiac glycosides — effective in oleander poisoning (Eddleston trial 2000 — reverses arrhythmias), though expensive and not widely available; fructose-1,6-diphosphate (experimental); continuous ECG monitoring for at least 24–72 h; (7) post-mortem — non-specific: congestion of organs, petechiae, gastric mucosal irritation with seeds/leaf fragments (identification), subendocardial haemorrhages; preserve stomach contents, viscera, blood; (8) medicolegal — yellow oleander: common suicidal poison in south India (Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh) and Sri Lanka (thousands of cases annually — especially young women), accidental poisoning in children (attractive fruits, flowers, seeds), homicidal rare; white oleander: suicidal, abortifacient (root inserted into the vagina/cervix as an abortion stick, or taken orally), cattle poisoning, homicidal (root in food — rare), folk remedies (leaf juice for skin diseases, snake bite); Cerbera odollam (pong-pong, 'othalanga' in Kerala) — contains cerberin — 'suicide tree' — used for suicide and homicide (difficult to detect with spices). (2) Aconite (monkshood, wolfsbane, 'mitha zahar' (sweet poison), 'bachnag', 'bish', 'bikh'): plants of the genus Aconitum (Ranunculaceae) — Aconitum napellus (European monkshood), Aconitum ferox (Indian aconite — grows in the Himalayas, Nepal, Sikkim — most toxic), Aconitum chasmanthum, A. heterophyllum (atis — non-toxic); all parts toxic, especially the root (tuberous, conical, dark brown externally, white starchy inside, shrivelled, with scars of rootlets), which resembles horseradish (Cochlearia armoracia) — accidental confusion; differences: aconite root — conical, dark brown, smaller, scars of rootlets, cut surface white turning pink on exposure, taste sweetish initially followed by tingling and numbness, odourless; horseradish root — cylindrical, long, buff/yellowish white, cut surface white (unchanged), pungent taste and odour (mustard-like), no numbness; toxic principle — aconitine (a highly toxic diterpenoid alkaloid — one of the most potent plant poisons), also pseudaconitine (in A. ferox — more toxic), indaconitine, bikhaconitine, benzaconine; aconitine is absorbed rapidly through mucous membranes and skin (handling roots); mechanism — binds to site 2 of voltage-gated sodium channels in the open state and causes persistent activation (slows inactivation) → membrane depolarisation, increased Na⁺ influx → repetitive firing, then conduction block in excitable tissues (nerves, myocardium, skeletal muscle); in heart — delayed afterdepolarisations, triggered activity → ventricular arrhythmias; also central vagal stimulation (bradycardia); fatal dose — 1 g of root (≈ 2–5 mg aconitine; 250 mg of A. ferox root); fatal period — 2–6 hours (may be minutes); clinical features — onset within minutes to 1–2 hours: (1) neurological — characteristic tingling (paraesthesia) and numbness starting in the mouth, lips, tongue and throat (burning), spreading to the face, then to limbs and whole body ('ants crawling', 'pins and needles'), followed by anaesthesia; muscle weakness, giddiness, blurred vision, diplopia, dilated or alternating pupils (pupils contract and dilate alternately — 'hippus'), headache, sweating, hypothermia; consciousness usually preserved till late; convulsions (rare); (2) gastrointestinal — salivation (excessive), nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea; (3) cardiovascular (cause of death) — hypotension, palpitations, chest pain, bradycardia, sinus tachycardia, ventricular ectopics, bigeminy, ventricular tachycardia (often polymorphic or bidirectional), torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation, asystole; arrhythmias are often resistant to treatment; (4) respiratory — dyspnoea, respiratory muscle paralysis; death due to ventricular arrhythmias or respiratory paralysis; diagnosis — history (Ayurvedic/Chinese herbal medicine — 'chuanwu', 'caowu', tinctures), characteristic paraesthesia + arrhythmias; LC-MS for aconitine in blood/urine; treatment — no specific antidote: gastric lavage, activated charcoal (early), atropine for bradycardia; anti-arrhythmics — amiodarone and flecainide (first-line for VT), lignocaine, procainamide, magnesium sulphate; cardioversion/defibrillation (often refractory); inotropes/vasopressors for hypotension; mechanical circulatory support (cardiopulmonary bypass, ECMO, IABP) until toxin is metabolised (24–48 h) — lifesaving in refractory cases; ventilation; post-mortem — non-specific (congestion of organs, asphyxial signs, gastric mucosa inflamed, root fragments in stomach); preserve viscera with stomach contents, blood, urine; aconitine is unstable (hydrolysed) — analyse early; medicolegal — historically a favoured homicidal poison (small fatal dose, can be mixed with food, symptoms resemble natural cardiac death, difficult to detect, few post-mortem findings) — the 'queen of poisons'; Lamson case (1881, England — aconitine given in cake to brother-in-law); accidental — use of improperly processed aconite in Ayurvedic and Chinese herbal medicines (detoxification by 'shodhana' — boiling in cow urine/milk; poorly processed 'bachnag' preparations), mistaken for horseradish/edible roots, handling roots (skin absorption); suicidal; arrow poison (hunting, Himalayan tribes), cattle poisoning (to kill tigers/animals — baits); (3) other cardiac poisons — digitalis (foxglove — digoxin, digitoxin; therapeutic overdose — xanthopsia, arrhythmias, hyperkalaemia; antidote — Fab), nicotine/tobacco (stimulation then depression of autonomic ganglia — vomiting, salivation, tachycardia then bradycardia, convulsions, respiratory paralysis; green tobacco sickness; nicotine e-liquid poisoning in children), cerbera (Cerbera odollam — Kerala 'suicide tree'), quinine (cinchonism, arrhythmias), calotropis (cardiac glycosides — calotropin).",
[["Yellow vs white oleander",[["Feature","Yellow oleander","White oleander"],["Botanical name","Thevetia peruviana (Cerbera thevetia)","Nerium odorum/indicum"],["Common name","Pila kaner","Safed kaner"],["Most toxic part","Seeds (kernels)","Root"],["Glycosides","Thevetin A, B, peruvoside, neriifolin","Oleandrin, neriodorin, neriodorein"],["Fatal dose","8–10 seeds","15–20 g root"],["Special","Suicide (south India, Sri Lanka)","Abortion stick, cattle poisoning; tetanic spasms"],["Mechanism","Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition (digoxin-like)","Same"]]],
["Oleander poisoning — features and treatment",[["Features","Treatment"],["Burning mouth, vomiting, diarrhoea","Lavage, MDAC"],["Bradycardia, PR prolongation, AV blocks, sinus arrest","Atropine, isoprenaline, temporary pacing"],["Ventricular ectopics, VT, VF","Lignocaine, phenytoin, Mg; low-energy cardioversion"],["Hyperkalaemia","Insulin–dextrose, bicarbonate; avoid calcium"],["Xanthopsia, confusion","Digoxin-specific Fab"]]],
["Aconite root vs horseradish",[["Feature","Aconite","Horseradish"],["Shape","Conical, dark brown, rootlet scars","Cylindrical, long, buff"],["Cut surface","White → pink","White, unchanged"],["Taste","Sweetish then tingling, numbness","Pungent"],["Odour","None","Pungent (mustard-like)"]]],
["Aconite — key facts",[["Feature","Detail"],["Toxin","Aconitine (pseudaconitine)"],["Mechanism","Persistent Na⁺ channel activation"],["Fatal dose","1 g root (2–5 mg aconitine)"],["Fatal period","2–6 h"],["Features","Tingling/numbness spreading from mouth, salivation, vomiting, hypotension, VT/VF"],["Treatment","Lavage, charcoal, atropine, amiodarone/flecainide, Mg, ECMO"],["Medicolegal","Historic homicidal poison; Ayurvedic 'bachnag' accidents; arrow poison"]]]],
{c:"Yellow and white oleanders contain digoxin-like cardiac glycosides that inhibit Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, causing vomiting, bradycardia, heart blocks, ventricular arrhythmias and hyperkalaemia, managed with multiple-dose charcoal, atropine, pacing and digoxin-specific Fab, while aconite's aconitine keeps sodium channels open, producing spreading tingling numbness and refractory ventricular arrhythmias needing anti-arrhythmics or ECMO; both feature in suicide, accidental herbal poisoning and historically homicide."});

Z("fmt9",11,"f",["Irritant plant poisons cause gastrointestinal inflammation and local tissue damage: castor (Ricinus communis — seeds contain ricin, a ribosome-inactivating toxalbumin; chewed seeds cause delayed haemorrhagic gastroenteritis, shock, hepatorenal failure), croton (Croton tiglium — jamalgota; croton oil and crotin — drastic purgative and vesicant), calotropis (madar/aak — milky latex with calotropin, uscharin — irritant, cardiotoxic; used for infanticide, abortion, cattle poisoning) and capsicum (chillies — capsaicin; burning irritation, used in robbery by throwing in eyes, in torture and for fabricating injuries); semecarpus (marking nut) also belongs here.","Irritant plants burn the gut or skin — castor and croton through toxic proteins and oils, calotropis through its milky latex, chilli through capsaicin's fire.",["Castor: seeds oval, glossy, mottled brown/grey (like a tick), caruncle at one end; ricin (A + B chains like abrin) + ricinine + agglutinin; castor oil itself non-toxic (ricin not extracted); fatal dose 6–10 chewed seeds (adult), 1–2 in children; features (latent hours): burning throat, nausea, vomiting, colic, bloody diarrhoea, dehydration, hypovolaemia, haemolysis, hepatic and renal failure, convulsions; death 2–3 days; treatment supportive; ricin bioterrorism (Markov umbrella murder 1978).","Croton: seeds resemble castor but dull, no caruncle; crotin (toxalbumin) + croton oil (resin — tiglic acid, phorbol esters — powerful purgative, vesicant, tumour promoter); fatal dose 4 seeds/20 drops oil; features — burning mouth, vomiting, violent purging (rice-water/bloody), collapse; on skin — vesication, pustules; uses — abortifacient, purgative quackery, cattle poisoning, homicide.","Calotropis (C. gigantea, C. procera — madar, aak): milky latex — calotropin, uscharin, calotoxin (cardiac glycosides), gigantin; local irritation, blisters (latex on skin/eyes — conjunctivitis, keratitis), ingestion — burning, salivation, vomiting, diarrhoea, bradycardia, convulsions; fatal period 6–8 h; uses — infanticide (latex put in mouth of newborn), abortion (latex on stick), cattle poisoning (latex mixed with fodder), suicide; fabrication of injuries (simulates bruises/burns).","Capsicum (Capsicum annuum/frutescens — red chilli): capsaicin (vanilloid TRPV1 agonist); ingestion — burning, gastritis, vomiting; eyes — intense pain, lacrimation, blepharospasm (temporary blindness — used by robbers); skin/genitals — burning, erythema (torture, punishment, 'chilli on genitals' of women in custody/witch-hunting); chilli seeds confused with datura seeds; pepper spray (OC).","Treatment (all): decontamination (lavage, charcoal for ingestion; washing skin/eyes with water/milk for chilli, latex), fluids, electrolytes, blood products, supportive organ care; PM — gastroenteritis, haemorrhages, seed fragments; preserve viscera, plant material; medicolegal — accidental (children), suicidal, homicidal (castor/croton rare), infanticide/abortion (calotropis), cattle poisoning, fabricated injuries (calotropis, semecarpus)."],"Castor — ricin; croton — purgative oil; calotropis — latex (infanticide, abortion); capsicum — capsaicin (robbery, torture)."],
"Vegetable (plant) irritant poisons produce local irritation and inflammation of the skin and mucous membranes, particularly of the gastrointestinal tract, causing vomiting, abdominal pain and purging; important ones include abrus (topic 9), castor, croton, calotropis, semecarpus, capsicum, ergot, colocynth, plumbago and jatropha. (1) Castor (Ricinus communis; 'arandi', 'erandi'): a shrub cultivated widely in India for castor oil; seeds — oval, slightly flattened, 1–1.5 cm, glossy, mottled with grey, brown and black (resembling a tick — 'ricinus' means tick), with a white fleshy caruncle at one end; toxic principles — ricin (a lectin/toxalbumin — type II ribosome-inactivating protein like abrin — B chain binds galactose receptors, A chain depurinates 28S rRNA and stops protein synthesis), ricinine (alkaloid — convulsant), ricinus agglutinin (haemagglutination); castor oil (cold-pressed from seeds) contains ricinoleic acid (purgative) but is free of ricin (water-soluble ricin remains in the seed cake/press cake; heat during processing destroys it); the seeds are toxic only when chewed/crushed (hard coat prevents release); fatal dose — 6–10 well-chewed seeds in adults (1–3 in children); purified ricin ~ 1–20 µg/kg (very potent — inhalation or injection more toxic); fatal period — 2–3 days (up to 5–7 days); clinical features (ingestion): latent period of a few hours (2–12 h; up to 24 h); burning in mouth and throat, nausea, persistent vomiting, severe colicky abdominal pain, profuse watery and then bloody diarrhoea, tenesmus, dehydration, thirst, hypovolaemic shock, drowsiness, weakness, cramps, haemolysis, haematuria, oliguria, hepatic necrosis, renal failure, convulsions, cyanosis, coma, death; inhalation of ricin (bioterrorism) — fever, cough, pulmonary oedema, ARDS within 24–72 h; injection — local necrosis, fever, multi-organ failure (Georgi Markov, Bulgarian dissident, killed in London 1978 by a ricin pellet fired from an umbrella); also allergic reactions (asthma, dermatitis) in castor oil mill workers; treatment — no antidote: gastric lavage/activated charcoal (early), aggressive fluid and electrolyte replacement, blood transfusion, alkalinisation of urine (haemoglobinuria), supportive care for hepatic/renal failure; post-mortem — haemorrhagic gastroenteritis, ulceration, seed fragments in stomach, petechiae, necrosis of liver, kidneys, lymphoid tissue; medicolegal — accidental (children chewing seeds — most common), suicidal, homicidal (rare), bioterrorism/assassination agent (Schedule 1 under Chemical Weapons Convention). (2) Croton (Croton tiglium; 'jamalgota', 'naepala'): a small tree of tropical Asia; seeds — oval, dull, dark brown/greyish, about the size of a coffee bean, with no caruncle (differs from castor seeds which are glossy and mottled with a caruncle), with longitudinal ridges; toxic principles — crotin (a toxalbumin similar to ricin) and croton oil (from seeds — contains crotonoside, tiglic acid and phorbol esters (e.g., 12-O-tetradecanoylphorbol-13-acetate — TPA) — a powerful vesicant, rubefacient, drastic purgative (hydragogue cathartic — one of the most potent purgatives), and tumour promoter); fatal dose — 4 seeds; croton oil 20 drops (1 ml); fatal period — 6 hours to 3 days; clinical features — ingestion: hot burning pain in mouth, throat and stomach, salivation, nausea, vomiting, violent abdominal colic, severe watery (rice-water) and later bloody diarrhoea (purging), tenesmus, dehydration, collapse, shock, cold clammy skin, feeble pulse, delirium, convulsions, death; skin — croton oil applied causes burning, redness, vesication (blisters), pustular eruption (used to fabricate injuries and as a counter-irritant); eyes — conjunctivitis; treatment — lavage, demulcents, fluids, electrolytes, supportive; post-mortem — acute gastroenteritis, haemorrhagic inflammation of GIT, ulcers; medicolegal — criminal abortion (seeds taken orally — violent purgation induces uterine contractions; oil applied on abortion stick), homicide (rare — added to food), quack remedies (purgative, aphrodisiac), cattle poisoning, fabricated injuries (skin vesication), accidental. (3) Calotropis (Calotropis gigantea — giant milkweed, 'madar'; Calotropis procera — 'aak'; Asclepiadaceae): common wild shrubs growing in dry wastelands across India; leaves thick, broad, greyish-green with whitish down; flowers lilac/purple-white (gigantea) or pink-white (procera); all parts exude a milky white acrid latex (juice) on injury; toxic principles (in latex and roots) — calotropin, uscharin, calotoxin, calactin (cardiac glycosides — digitalis-like), gigantin, mudarine; latex also contains proteolytic enzymes (calotropain) — highly irritant; clinical features — local: latex on skin causes redness, vesication, blistering, dermatitis; in eyes — severe conjunctivitis, keratitis, corneal oedema, ulceration (accidental 'calotropis keratitis'); ingestion: bitter acrid taste, burning pain in mouth and throat, salivation, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, stomatitis, gastroenteritis, dilated pupils, tetanic convulsions, bradycardia, arrhythmias (cardiac glycoside effects), collapse, death; fatal dose — uncertain; fatal period — about 6–12 hours; treatment — gastric lavage, demulcents, symptomatic, treatment of arrhythmias as for oleander (atropine, Fab), eye irrigation and topical steroids/antibiotics; post-mortem — inflammation of GIT, congestion; latex may be detected; medicolegal — (a) infanticide — the latex is put into the mouth of a newborn baby (or the plant crushed and given) to kill it (female infanticide in some regions — reported historically); (b) criminal abortion — latex or a stick smeared with latex inserted into the uterus/vagina ('abortion stick'), or the root paste taken orally; (c) cattle poisoning (latex mixed with fodder, chopped leaves, or applied on 'sui'); (d) suicide (latex ingestion); (e) homicide (rare); (f) fabrication of injuries — latex applied to skin produces blisters/bruise-like marks to support false charges of assault (differentiated from real bruises — no swelling/tenderness, vesication, history); (g) folk medicine — toothache, skin diseases, snake bite, as a purgative; (h) accidental eye injuries. (4) Capsicum (Capsicum annuum, Capsicum frutescens — red chilli pepper, 'lal mirch'): the fruits and seeds are used as spice; active principle — capsaicin (and capsicin) — a vanilloid compound that activates TRPV1 receptors on nociceptive (C) fibres causing burning pain, neurogenic inflammation (substance P release), followed by desensitisation (used therapeutically in topical analgesic creams); oleoresin capsicum is used in pepper sprays (self-defence, riot control); effects — ingestion of large quantities: intense burning in mouth, throat and stomach, salivation, lacrimation, sweating, gastritis, vomiting, diarrhoea with burning anal sensation (rarely dangerous in adults; aspiration and laryngeal spasm/airway oedema in children); eyes — intense burning pain, lacrimation, blepharospasm (involuntary closure of eyelids — temporary inability to see), conjunctival congestion, corneal abrasions — chilli powder thrown into the eyes is a common method used by robbers to incapacitate victims ('mirchi attack'); skin — burning, erythema, vesication; mucous membranes (nose, genitals, anus) — severe burning; torture — chilli powder applied to genitals/anus or eyes of victims (custodial torture, punishment of women accused of witchcraft, domestic violence, ragging), or rubbed on breasts; inhalation — cough, bronchospasm; treatment — decontamination: wash eyes with copious water/saline (± milk), skin with soap and water, vegetable oil/milk or yoghurt (capsaicin is fat-soluble), cool compresses; analgesics; topical anaesthetic for eyes; steroid/antibiotic drops for corneal injuries; bronchodilators; medicolegal — robbery (throwing in eyes), torture and humiliation (S. 117–118 BNS — hurt/grievous hurt by corrosive/heated substances; custodial torture — S. 120), punishment of children (S. 75 JJ Act — cruelty), pepper spray (riot control), confusion of chilli seeds with datura seeds (identification); adulterants (brick powder, Sudan dyes — food adulteration). (5) Semecarpus anacardium (marking nut, 'bhilawa', 'bhallataka'): the black corrosive juice of the pericarp (contains bhilawanol, anacardic acid, semecarpol) is a powerful vesicant used by washermen to mark clothes (indelible ink); applied on skin it produces painful blisters and bruise-like lesions — used to fabricate injuries (difference from true bruises: dark irregular blister with acrid juice, itching, eczema, no swelling of underlying tissue, satellite lesions from fingers, juice-stained fingers of applier), for criminal abortion (juice on stick), cattle poisoning; ingestion causes gastroenteritis, haematuria, delirium. (6) Others — ergot (Claviceps purpurea fungus on rye — abortifacient; ergotism — gangrene, convulsions), colocynth (Citrullus colocynthis — drastic purgative), plumbago (Plumbago rosea/zeylanica — 'chitra' — root paste on abortion stick), jatropha (Jatropha curcas — physic nut — curcin; children — gastroenteritis), gloriosa (Gloriosa superba — colchicine — suicidal in south India — multi-organ failure, alopecia), cleistanthus (oduvan — Tamil Nadu suicidal poison), cerbera.",
[["Irritant plant poisons",[["Plant","Toxic principle","Key features","Medicolegal"],["Castor (Ricinus communis)","Ricin (RIP), ricinine","Delayed haemorrhagic gastroenteritis, shock, hepatorenal failure","Accidental (children), bioterrorism (Markov)"],["Croton (Croton tiglium)","Crotin, croton oil (phorbol esters)","Violent purging, collapse; skin vesication","Abortion, homicide, fabricated injury"],["Calotropis (madar/aak)","Calotropin, uscharin (cardiac glycosides), latex","Local blisters, keratitis; GI irritation, bradycardia, convulsions","Infanticide, abortion, cattle poisoning, fabricated injury"],["Capsicum (chilli)","Capsaicin (TRPV1)","Burning, lacrimation, blepharospasm","Robbery (eyes), torture"],["Semecarpus (marking nut)","Bhilawanol (vesicant)","Blisters simulating bruises","Fabricated injury, abortion"]]],
["Castor seeds vs croton seeds",[["Feature","Castor","Croton"],["Surface","Glossy, mottled (tick-like)","Dull, dark brown/grey"],["Caruncle","Present","Absent"],["Toxin","Ricin","Crotin + croton oil"],["Fatal dose","6–10 chewed seeds","4 seeds / 20 drops oil"]]],
["Calotropis — medicolegal uses",["Infanticide (latex in newborn's mouth)","Criminal abortion (latex on stick)","Cattle poisoning","Suicide","Fabricated injuries (blisters)"]],
["Treatment (general)",["Lavage/charcoal for recent ingestion","Fluids, electrolytes, blood products","Supportive organ care (liver, kidney)","Eyes/skin: copious water; milk/oil for chilli","Cardiac glycoside management for calotropis"]]],
{c:"Irritant plant poisons include castor seeds whose ricin causes delayed haemorrhagic gastroenteritis and organ failure, croton whose oil is a drastic purgative and vesicant, calotropis whose milky latex irritates, blisters and has digitalis-like effects and is used for infanticide, abortion and cattle poisoning, and capsicum whose capsaicin is weaponised in robberies and torture; management is decontamination and supportive care."});

Z("fmt9",12,"f",["Kerosene and other hydrocarbons (petrol, turpentine, diesel, mineral oils, solvents) are common accidental poisons in children; their main danger is aspiration into the lungs because of low viscosity, low surface tension and high volatility, causing chemical pneumonitis (cough, dyspnoea, fever, crepitations, infiltrates) rather than systemic toxicity; CNS depression, cardiac sensitisation to catecholamines (sudden sniffing death with volatile solvents) and GI irritation also occur; gastric lavage and emetics are contraindicated, and treatment is supportive (oxygen, ventilation), with antibiotics and steroids not routinely indicated.","Kerosene's real threat is not the stomach but the lungs — a few drops slip into the airway and inflame them, so never make the child vomit.",["Physical properties determining aspiration: low viscosity (< 60 SUS), low surface tension, high volatility — kerosene, petrol, turpentine, mineral seal oil; high viscosity (diesel, lubricating oil, paraffin) — low aspiration risk; aromatic (benzene, toluene, xylene) and halogenated (CCl₄, trichloroethylene) — systemic toxicity (bone marrow, liver, kidney, CNS).","Features: burning mouth, cough, choking, gagging immediately (aspiration), tachypnoea, dyspnoea, wheeze, cyanosis, fever (chemical pneumonitis, not infection), crepitations; CXR — basal infiltrates within 6 h (perihilar, lower lobes), pneumatoceles, pneumothorax; vomiting, abdominal pain; CNS — drowsiness, ataxia, convulsions, coma; arrhythmias (myocardial sensitisation — sudden death with exertion/fright in sniffers); kerosene smell.","Treatment: no emesis, no lavage (unless large ingestion of highly toxic hydrocarbon with toxic additive — e.g., OP in kerosene — with cuffed ET tube); remove contaminated clothes, wash skin; oxygen, observe 6 h (CXR at 6 h); respiratory support (CPAP, ventilation, surfactant, ECMO); avoid adrenaline (arrhythmias); antibiotics only if secondary infection; steroids not useful; activated charcoal not useful.","Inhalant abuse (glue sniffing, petrol, toluene, 'whitener', correction fluid, nail polish remover): euphoria, hallucinations, ataxia; 'sudden sniffing death syndrome' — VF; chronic — cerebellar degeneration, neuropathy (n-hexane), renal tubular acidosis (toluene), leukaemia (benzene), 'glue sniffer's rash' around mouth; children of street.","PM: kerosene smell, froth, chemical pneumonitis (haemorrhagic, oedematous lungs, consolidation), gastric irritation; preserve viscera in airtight containers without rectified spirit (salt or none), lungs and brain in sealed nylon bags; medicolegal — accidental (children; kerosene stored in soft drink bottles), suicidal (ingestion; self-immolation with kerosene), homicidal (bride burning — kerosene poured and set on fire), kerosene as vehicle for pesticides."],"Kerosene: aspiration pneumonitis; no emesis or lavage; supportive; sudden sniffing death with solvents."],
"Hydrocarbons are organic compounds made of hydrogen and carbon, derived mainly from petroleum (petroleum distillates) or pine wood (turpentine) or coal tar; classification — (1) aliphatic (straight-chain) petroleum distillates — kerosene (mitti ka tel — 'paraffin oil' in UK), petrol (gasoline), naphtha, mineral spirits (white spirit), lighter fluid, diesel, mineral seal oil (furniture polish), lubricating oils, paraffin wax, Vaseline; (2) aromatic hydrocarbons — benzene, toluene, xylene (solvents, paints, glues, thinners); (3) halogenated hydrocarbons — carbon tetrachloride, chloroform, trichloroethylene, methylene chloride, Freons; (4) terpenes — turpentine, pine oil; (5) others — camphor, naphthalene (mothballs), gasoline additives (tetraethyl lead). Kerosene is the commonest cause of hydrocarbon poisoning in India, especially among children 1–5 years in lower socioeconomic homes (used as fuel for cooking and lighting, stored in unlabelled soft-drink bottles or cups within reach); it is a colourless/pale yellow liquid with characteristic odour, a mixture of C₁₀–C₁₆ hydrocarbons; also the vehicle (solvent) for many insecticides (OP compounds). Toxicity depends on physical properties: (1) viscosity — the most important determinant of aspiration risk; low viscosity (< 60 Saybolt Seconds Universal (SSU); kerosene ~ 35, petrol ~ 30, turpentine, mineral seal oil) liquids spread easily over a large surface and penetrate deep into the airways; high-viscosity products (> 100 SSU — lubricating oils, diesel, petroleum jelly, paraffin wax) have low aspiration risk; (2) surface tension — low surface tension allows rapid spread along mucosal surfaces into the tracheobronchial tree; (3) volatility — high volatility allows inhalation of vapours, displacement of alveolar oxygen (hypoxia), and CNS absorption; (4) chemical nature — aliphatic hydrocarbons are poorly absorbed from GIT and have low systemic toxicity (the danger is pulmonary), whereas aromatic and halogenated hydrocarbons are well absorbed and cause systemic toxicity (CNS depression, cardiac arrhythmias, hepatotoxicity — CCl₄, chloroform; nephrotoxicity — toluene (renal tubular acidosis), CCl₄; bone marrow toxicity — benzene (aplastic anaemia, leukaemia); peripheral neuropathy — n-hexane, methyl-n-butyl ketone); (5) additives — pesticides, heavy metals, camphor increase toxicity ('CHAMP' mnemonic for hydrocarbons warranting decontamination: Camphor, Halogenated, Aromatic, Metals, Pesticides). Mechanism of pulmonary toxicity: even a small amount (< 1 ml) aspirated during swallowing, choking or vomiting spreads over alveolar surfaces, dissolving and inactivating surfactant (alveolar collapse, atelectasis, V/Q mismatch, hypoxaemia), causing direct injury to alveolar and capillary membranes (chemical/lipid pneumonitis — inflammation, oedema, haemorrhage, hyaline membranes, necrotising bronchopneumonia), bronchospasm; later pneumatoceles, pneumothorax, pleural effusion, secondary bacterial infection; ARDS. Fatal dose — kerosene: ~ 30–60 ml (adults orally; far less if aspirated); in children 10–30 ml; fatal period — 24 h (may be several days from pneumonia). Clinical features: (1) respiratory (most important; in up to 40% of children; appears within 30 min to 6 hours) — immediate coughing, choking, gagging (suggests aspiration), burning in mouth and throat, tachypnoea, dyspnoea, grunting, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, wheeze, cyanosis, crepitations/rales, decreased breath sounds; fever (often high — 38–40 °C — due to chemical pneumonitis, not necessarily infection) for 1–3 days; haemoptysis, pulmonary oedema; chest X-ray — abnormalities appear within 2–6 hours (may lag behind symptoms; normal X-ray at 6 h in an asymptomatic child suggests no significant aspiration) — bilateral perihilar/basal infiltrates, patchy opacities, consolidation (lower lobes, right middle lobe), pneumatoceles (days–weeks), pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, pleural effusion; (2) gastrointestinal — burning, nausea, vomiting (increases risk of aspiration), abdominal pain, diarrhoea; kerosene odour of breath and vomit; (3) CNS — lethargy, drowsiness, irritability, dizziness, headache, ataxia, convulsions, coma (due to hypoxia from pneumonitis, or direct CNS depression with aromatic/halogenated compounds and inhalants); (4) cardiovascular — myocardial sensitisation to endogenous catecholamines (especially halogenated and aromatic hydrocarbons, petrol vapours) → ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF) — 'sudden sniffing death syndrome' when an inhalant abuser is startled or exerts himself; (5) others — skin contact: dermatitis, defatting, chemical burns (prolonged contact — petrol, kerosene), hydrocarbon injection injuries (high-pressure guns — compartment syndrome); eyes — irritation; haemolysis, renal and hepatic dysfunction (rare with kerosene); fever, leucocytosis. Diagnosis: history and kerosene smell, respiratory signs, chest X-ray at presentation and 6 h; pulse oximetry, ABG; CBC; ECG. Management: (1) do not induce vomiting (no ipecac, no salt water, no finger in throat) — emesis increases aspiration risk; (2) gastric lavage is contraindicated in kerosene/petroleum distillate poisoning (aspiration risk) — the only exceptions are ingestion of large volumes of hydrocarbons with systemic toxicity (e.g., benzene, CCl₄) or containing dangerous additives (pesticides like OP in kerosene, heavy metals, camphor) — then performed cautiously after intubation with a cuffed endotracheal tube; (3) activated charcoal — not useful (poor adsorption, induces vomiting); (4) decontamination — remove contaminated clothing, wash skin with soap and water, irrigate eyes; health workers protect themselves; (5) observation — asymptomatic patients with normal chest X-ray and oxygen saturation at 6 hours can be discharged with advice; symptomatic patients admitted; (6) respiratory support — humidified oxygen, bronchodilators (salbutamol) for bronchospasm, CPAP/high-flow oxygen, mechanical ventilation with PEEP for respiratory failure, surfactant therapy, high-frequency oscillatory ventilation, ECMO in severe ARDS; (7) antibiotics — not routinely (fever and leucocytosis are common in chemical pneumonitis); only for documented or suspected secondary bacterial infection (after 48–72 h, new fever, sputum); (8) corticosteroids — not recommended (no benefit; may increase infection); (9) avoid adrenaline and other sympathomimetics (risk of arrhythmias due to myocardial sensitisation) — use β-blockers (esmolol, propranolol) for ventricular arrhythmias; (10) supportive — fluids, temperature control, anticonvulsants; (11) prevention — child-resistant containers, not storing kerosene in drink bottles, safe storage, switch to LPG (Ujjwala scheme), education. Inhalant/volatile substance abuse (solvent abuse): intentional inhalation of volatile solvents for euphoria — sniffing (from container), huffing (from soaked cloth), bagging (from bag); substances — glues (toluene), correction fluid/whitener ('Eraz-ex', 'Tipp-Ex' — trichloroethane), thinners, petrol, aerosols (propellants — butane, propane, fluorocarbons), lighter fluid, nail polish remover (acetone), paint, adhesive, nitrites ('poppers'), nitrous oxide; common among street children and adolescents in India; acute effects — rapid euphoria, disinhibition, excitement, hallucinations, slurred speech, ataxia, then drowsiness, stupor; dangers — sudden sniffing death syndrome (ventricular fibrillation due to cardiac sensitisation, especially with exertion or fright), asphyxia (plastic bag over head, displacement of oxygen), aspiration of vomit, accidents, burns (flammable), laryngospasm (cold aerosols); chronic — cerebral and cerebellar atrophy (toluene leukoencephalopathy — ataxia, dementia, nystagmus), peripheral neuropathy (n-hexane — glue; methyl n-butyl ketone), renal tubular acidosis with hypokalaemia (toluene), hepatotoxicity (halogenated — CCl₄), bone marrow aplasia and leukaemia (benzene), methaemoglobinaemia (nitrites), vitamin B₁₂ inactivation and myeloneuropathy (nitrous oxide); 'glue sniffer's rash' (perioral eczema), paint stains on face/hands, solvent odour on breath; legal — sale of whiteners to minors restricted in some states; treatment — supportive, avoid catecholamines, rehabilitation. Post-mortem findings (kerosene poisoning): external — kerosene odour from mouth, nostrils and body cavities, froth at mouth/nostrils, cyanosis; skin burns/irritation around mouth (contact); internal — oedematous, haemorrhagic, heavy, consolidated lungs (chemical pneumonitis — patches of haemorrhagic consolidation, especially in lower lobes; froth in airways with kerosene smell), hyaline membranes, emphysema, pleural effusion; gastric mucosa congested, with kerosene-smelling contents floating as an oily layer; congestion of brain and other organs; signs of hypoxia; in inhalant deaths — few findings (arrhythmic death), solvent smell in lungs and brain; samples — stomach and contents, intestine, liver, kidney, blood, lungs and brain — volatile substances should be collected in airtight glass containers filled completely or sealed nylon bags (to prevent evaporation), stored refrigerated; preservative — saturated salt solution or no preservative; rectified spirit must not be used (interferes with analysis of volatile hydrocarbons); analysis by headspace gas chromatography (GC-MS). Medicolegal aspects: (1) accidental poisoning — most common, in children (kerosene stored in soft drink bottles, cups, mistaken for water/drink), siphoning petrol by mouth ('gasoline siphoner's lung'), fire-eaters ('fire-eater's pneumonia' — kerosene/paraffin aspiration by street performers), occupational exposure; (2) suicidal — ingestion of kerosene (alone or with insecticides), and more often self-immolation — pouring kerosene over the body and setting fire (burn deaths; dowry-related); (3) homicidal — kerosene ingestion forced in children (rare); bride burning — kerosene poured on the victim and set on fire (dowry deaths — S. 80 BNS; kerosene smell on clothes/body parts like back and genitals, container at scene, injuries, burns on assailant); (4) kerosene as a vehicle for pesticides (smell may mislead); (5) inhalant abuse among children — exploitation, crime, accidental deaths; (6) adulteration of diesel/petrol with kerosene (subsidised kerosene diversion — economic offence).",
[["Factors determining hydrocarbon toxicity",[["Factor","High risk"],["Viscosity","Low (kerosene, petrol, turpentine) → aspiration"],["Surface tension","Low → spreads into airways"],["Volatility","High → inhalation, CNS effects"],["Chemical type","Aromatic/halogenated → systemic toxicity"],["Additives (CHAMP)","Camphor, Halogenated, Aromatic, Metals, Pesticides → decontaminate"]]],
["Clinical features",[["System","Features"],["Respiratory","Cough, choking, tachypnoea, wheeze, cyanosis, fever, crepitations; CXR infiltrates by 6 h; pneumatoceles"],["GIT","Burning, vomiting, abdominal pain; kerosene odour"],["CNS","Drowsiness, convulsions, coma"],["CVS","Myocardial sensitisation → VF (sudden sniffing death)"],["Skin","Dermatitis, burns"]]],
["Management",[["Do","Don't"],["Remove clothes, wash skin","Induce vomiting"],["Oxygen; observe 6 h; CXR","Gastric lavage (except CHAMP with cuffed tube)"],["Bronchodilators, CPAP, ventilation, ECMO","Activated charcoal"],["Antibiotics only if infection","Routine steroids"],["β-blockers for arrhythmias","Adrenaline/sympathomimetics"]]],
["Inhalant abuse",[["Substance","Chronic toxicity"],["Toluene (glue)","Cerebellar atrophy, renal tubular acidosis"],["n-Hexane","Peripheral neuropathy"],["Benzene","Aplastic anaemia, leukaemia"],["CCl₄","Hepatorenal toxicity"],["Nitrous oxide","B₁₂ inactivation, myeloneuropathy"],["All","Sudden sniffing death (VF)"]]],
["Medicolegal",["Accidental — children (kerosene in drink bottles); fire-eaters","Suicidal ingestion; self-immolation","Homicidal — bride burning (S. 80 BNS)","Vehicle for pesticides","Viscera: airtight, no rectified spirit"]]],
{c:"Kerosene and other low-viscosity hydrocarbons are dangerous chiefly because tiny aspirated amounts cause chemical pneumonitis with cough, hypoxia, fever and infiltrates, while aromatic and halogenated solvents add systemic, cardiac and organ toxicity, including sudden sniffing death; emesis and lavage are contraindicated, care is supportive, and medicolegally kerosene features in childhood accidents, suicides and bride burning."});

Z("fmt9",13,"f",["Dhatura, cannabis and alcohol intoxication all cause altered behaviour and incoordination but differ: dhatura (anticholinergic deliriant) gives dry hot flushed skin, dilated non-reacting pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention and true delirium with carphology and later amnesia; cannabis gives euphoria, laughter, distortion of time and space, conjunctival injection with normal/slightly dilated reacting pupils, hunger and drowsiness with preserved memory of a 'high'; alcohol gives smell of alcohol, flushed moist skin, nystagmus, slurred speech, staggering gait, normal/dilated reacting pupils, vomiting and progressive CNS depression measurable by blood alcohol.","Dhatura dries and fires up (hot, dry, wide pupils, delirium); cannabis lightens and distorts (red eyes, laughter, time slows); alcohol dulls and staggers (smell, slurred speech, nystagmus).",["Skin: dhatura — hot, dry, flushed, scarlatiniform rash; cannabis — normal; alcohol — flushed, moist/sweaty.","Pupils: dhatura — widely dilated, fixed, non-reacting; cannabis — normal or slightly dilated, reacting, conjunctival injection (red eyes); alcohol — normal/dilated, reacting; nystagmus.","Mental state: dhatura — delirium, hallucinations, incoherent speech, picking at clothes, thread pulling, aggression, complete amnesia; cannabis — euphoria, uncontrollable laughter, talkativeness, distorted time/space, hallucinations, panic/psychosis in excess, memory partly retained; alcohol — euphoria → disinhibition → confusion, emotional lability, slurred speech, ataxia, stupor; amnesia (blackouts) in heavy drinking.","Other signs: dhatura — dry mouth, dysphagia, hoarse voice, tachycardia, hyperthermia, urinary retention; cannabis — dry mouth, tachycardia, hunger, drowsiness; alcohol — smell of breath, vomiting, diuresis, hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, bradycardia/hypotension in deep coma.","Tests and treatment: dhatura — seeds in vomit, cat's eye test, physostigmine; cannabis — urine THC-COOH, Duquenois–Levine, reassurance/benzodiazepines; alcohol — breath/blood alcohol (Widmark), supportive, thiamine, glucose; medicolegal — dhatura (road poisoning, robbery), cannabis (run amok, NDPS), alcohol (drunken driving, crimes; voluntary intoxication no defence)."],"Dhatura: hot, dry, wide fixed pupils; cannabis: red eyes, laughter; alcohol: smell, nystagmus, staggering."],
"Persons presenting with acute disturbance of behaviour, incoordination and altered consciousness may be intoxicated with dhatura (datura), cannabis or alcohol (or a combination — e.g., bhang mixed with datura, alcohol laced with sedatives); differentiation is important for treatment (physostigmine for datura, supportive care for cannabis, thiamine/glucose and airway care for alcohol) and for medicolegal opinion (road poisoning, criminal responsibility, drunk driving, NDPS offences). (1) Mechanism and class — datura: tropane alkaloids (hyoscine, hyoscyamine, atropine) — antimuscarinic deliriant (cerebral poison); cannabis: Δ⁹-THC acting on CB1 receptors — euphoriant/hallucinogenic deliriant; alcohol (ethanol): GABA-A potentiation and NMDA inhibition — CNS depressant (inebriant). (2) Onset and duration — datura: 15–30 min, delirium lasting hours to 1–2 days; cannabis: smoking — minutes, oral (bhang) 30 min–2 h, lasting 3–12 h; alcohol: 15–60 min, depends on amount (elimination ~ 15–20 mg/dl/h). (3) Odour of breath — datura: none characteristic; cannabis: smoke odour (if smoked), otherwise none; alcohol: characteristic alcoholic smell (due to congeners — note: smell indicates consumption, not intoxication). (4) Face and skin — datura: face flushed, hot, dry (absence of sweating — dry skin, dry axillae), scarlatiniform erythematous rash over face, neck and upper chest; hyperthermia; cannabis: face may be flushed, skin normal; alcohol: face flushed, skin warm and moist (cutaneous vasodilatation, sweating), later pale, cold, clammy in deep intoxication (hypothermia). (5) Eyes — datura: pupils widely dilated (mydriasis), fixed, not reacting to light, blurred vision (cycloplegia), photophobia, conjunctivae may be congested; cannabis: conjunctival injection (bloodshot/red eyes) — characteristic; pupils normal or slightly dilated, reacting to light; ptosis; alcohol: conjunctivae congested, pupils normal or slightly dilated (constricted in deep coma), reacting to light (sluggish), horizontal gaze nystagmus (characteristic — onset of nystagmus at < 45° indicates BAC > 100 mg/dl), diplopia. (6) Mouth and throat — datura: intense dryness of mouth and throat, thirst, difficulty in swallowing (dysphagia), hoarse husky voice, dryness of tongue; cannabis: dry mouth, increased appetite ('munchies'); alcohol: moist mouth, vomiting common. (7) Pulse and BP — datura: tachycardia (rapid, bounding), hypertension; cannabis: tachycardia, postural hypotension; alcohol: initially rapid, later slow; hypotension in deep coma. (8) Temperature — datura: raised (hyperpyrexia); cannabis: normal or slightly lowered; alcohol: lowered (hypothermia, especially in cold weather). (9) Mental state and behaviour — datura: true delirium — restlessness, confusion, disorientation, incoherent speech, vivid visual hallucinations, illusions, purposeless movements (picking at bedclothes — carphology; pulling imaginary threads — floccillation; trying to catch imaginary objects), grasping, mimicking actions (as if smoking, stitching), aggression, violence, maniacal excitement; later drowsiness, stupor, coma, convulsions; complete amnesia for the period of intoxication on recovery; cannabis: euphoria ('high'), feeling of well-being, uncontrollable laughter, talkativeness, heightened sensory perception, distortion of time (time slowed — minutes seem hours) and space (distances magnified), depersonalisation, illusions and hallucinations, impaired short-term memory and judgement, anxiety, panic, paranoia and acute psychosis with large doses ('run amok' — homicidal frenzy), followed by drowsiness and sleep; the person usually remembers the experience; alcohol: stages — excitement (euphoria, talkativeness, disinhibition, loss of self-control, aggressive or amorous behaviour, impaired judgement), incoordination (confusion, emotional lability, slurred speech, ataxia — staggering gait, Romberg positive, inability to walk heel-to-toe, impaired finger–nose test), stupor/coma; memory blackouts in heavy intoxication; hypoglycaemia (especially children), aspiration. (10) Speech — datura: incoherent, rambling, hoarse; cannabis: talkative, relevant but rapid; alcohol: slurred, thick (dysarthria). (11) Gait — datura: staggering, ataxic ('drunken'); cannabis: mild incoordination; alcohol: staggering, broad-based, unsteady. (12) Urinary bladder — datura: urinary retention (distended bladder); cannabis: normal; alcohol: diuresis (ADH inhibition) — frequent urination, incontinence in stupor. (13) Bowel — datura: reduced bowel sounds (ileus); others normal. (14) Investigations — datura: seeds in vomitus/stomach wash (kidney-shaped, thick ridge), mydriatic (cat's eye) test, Vitali's test, GC-MS for atropine/hyoscine in urine; response to physostigmine; cannabis: urinary THC-COOH by immunoassay (detectable days to weeks), Duquenois–Levine colour test on material, GC-MS; alcohol: breath alcohol analyser, blood alcohol concentration (GC — headspace), urine/vitreous alcohol; correlation of BAC with signs (Widmark's formula for back-calculation). (15) Treatment — datura: lavage (even late), activated charcoal, physostigmine (1–2 mg IV slowly), benzodiazepines, cooling, catheterisation; avoid phenothiazines/antihistamines; cannabis: reassurance in a quiet environment ('talk down'), benzodiazepines for anxiety/agitation, antipsychotics for psychosis, observation; alcohol: airway protection, recovery position (prevent aspiration), IV fluids, dextrose (after thiamine 100 mg), warmth, haemodialysis in severe toxicity. (16) Prognosis — datura: usually recovery in 24–48 h if treated; deaths in children/large doses; cannabis: practically never fatal alone; alcohol: fatal at high BAC (> 400–500 mg/dl), aspiration, hypothermia, trauma. (17) Medicolegal — datura: road/railway poisoning (stupefaction for robbery — S. 123 BNS), kidnapping, rape (victim's amnesia), accidental poisoning (children, confusion with chilli seeds), mixed in bhang; cannabis: NDPS Act (ganja, charas prohibited; bhang regulated by state excise), crimes and accidents under intoxication, run amok, impaired driving (MV Act S. 185 — drugs); alcohol: drunkenness and medical examination (consent; S. 51 BNSS), drunken driving (MV Act S. 185 — BAC > 30 mg/100 ml), criminal responsibility (voluntary intoxication is not a defence for crimes requiring knowledge — S. 23 BNS / 85–86 IPC; involuntary intoxication is a defence), civil (contracts, wills under intoxication), deaths (accidents, hypothermia, aspiration), state prohibition laws; differentiation from head injury, hypoglycaemia, diabetic coma, hepatic encephalopathy, other poisoning (always consider head injury in an apparently drunk person — the smell of alcohol must not mislead).",
[["Dhatura vs cannabis vs alcohol",[["Feature","Dhatura","Cannabis","Alcohol"],["Class","Anticholinergic deliriant","Euphoriant (THC)","CNS depressant"],["Breath odour","None","Smoke (if smoked)","Alcoholic"],["Skin","Hot, dry, flushed, rash","Normal","Flushed, moist (later cold)"],["Temperature","Raised","Normal","Lowered"],["Pupils","Widely dilated, fixed","Normal/slightly dilated, reacting","Normal/dilated, reacting"],["Eyes — other","Blurred vision, photophobia","Red, injected conjunctivae","Congested; nystagmus"],["Mouth","Very dry, dysphagia, hoarse voice","Dry, hunger","Moist, vomiting"],["Pulse","Rapid, bounding","Rapid","Rapid then slow"],["Mental state","True delirium, hallucinations, carphology","Euphoria, laughter, time/space distortion","Disinhibition → confusion → stupor"],["Speech","Incoherent","Talkative","Slurred"],["Bladder","Retention","Normal","Diuresis"],["Memory","Complete amnesia","Retained","Blackouts in heavy use"],["Test","Seeds, cat's eye test, GC-MS","Urine THC-COOH, Duquenois–Levine","Breath/blood alcohol"],["Treatment","Physostigmine, benzodiazepines, cooling","Reassurance, benzodiazepines","Airway, thiamine, glucose, warmth"],["Medicolegal","Road poisoning, robbery","NDPS, run amok","Drunken driving, crimes"]]],
["Pitfalls",["Smell of alcohol ≠ intoxication","Always exclude head injury, hypoglycaemia","Mixed intoxications (bhang + datura)","Datura victims cannot identify culprits (amnesia)"]]],
{c:"Dhatura intoxication is distinguished by the anticholinergic picture of hot dry flushed skin, fixed dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention and true delirium with later amnesia, cannabis by euphoria, laughter, time distortion and red eyes with reactive pupils and retained memory, and alcohol by its odour, moist flushed skin, nystagmus, slurred speech and staggering gait confirmed by blood alcohol, each with its own treatment and medicolegal implications."});
